(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["bst",{"l:bst/how-does-a-binary-search-tree-find-and-insert-a-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A binary search tree (BST) keeps one \u003cb>invariant\u003c/b>: every key in a node's left subtree is smaller than the node's key, and every key in its right subtree is larger. So one comparison at each node tells you which subtree can contain the key, and search, insert, minimum and maximum each walk a \u003cb>single root-to-node path\u003c/b>: O(h) time, where h is the height. Range queries prune whole subtrees the same way.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Play \"guess the number\" with a friend who answers \"higher\" or \"lower\". Binary search on a sorted array does that with indices. A BST does it with pointers: the root is your first guess, and \"lower\" sends you to the left subtree, which is itself a smaller guessing game. The difference from a sorted array is what happens on insert: the array must shift everything after the new key, while the tree just hangs a new leaf where the failed search ended.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>           40\n         /    \\\n       20      60          left subtree of 40: {10, 20, 30}, all &lt; 40\n      /  \\    /  \\         right subtree of 40: {50, 60, 70}, all &gt; 40\n    10   30  50   70       the same holds at every node\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The invariant and what it buys\u003c/h5>\u003cp>For every node x: all keys in \u003ccode>x.left\u003c/code> are less than \u003ccode>x.val\u003c/code>, and all keys in \u003ccode>x.right\u003c/code> are greater. (This module assumes distinct keys; a multiset stores counts in the node, or sends equal keys consistently to one side.) The invariant is about whole subtrees, not just children, and that is what makes the next step safe: if the key you want is smaller than \u003ccode>x.val\u003c/code>, it cannot be anywhere in the right subtree, so you may forget the right subtree entirely.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Search\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Start at the root. If the key equals the node's key, stop. If it is smaller go left, otherwise go right. Reaching None means the key is absent, and the None you reached is exactly where it would be inserted.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Insert\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Search for the key; when you fall off the tree, attach a new node there. Written recursively, \u003ccode>insert(node, key)\u003c/code> \u003cb>returns the root of the updated subtree\u003c/b>, and each parent reassigns its child pointer: \u003ccode>node.left = insert(node.left, key)\u003c/code>. That \"return the new subtree root\" pattern makes the empty-tree case and the attach step the same line, and the next card (deletion) uses it again.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Minimum, maximum and the split point\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The minimum is the leftmost node (go left until you cannot), the maximum the rightmost. For two keys p &lt; q, the \u003cb>split point\u003c/b> is the first node on the path from the root where they stop going the same way: p goes left (or is the node) and q goes right (or is the node). Every node above it has both keys on one side, and the split node has them on different sides, so it is their lowest common ancestor. In a plain binary tree finding it needed a full traversal; here it is one path.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Range queries prune subtrees\u003c/h5>\u003cp>To sum (or list) the keys in [lo, hi]: at a node smaller than lo, the whole left subtree is smaller still, so only the right subtree can contribute; symmetrically for a node larger than hi; a node inside the range contributes itself and both subtrees. You visit the k keys in range plus the nodes on the two boundary paths.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each step of search, insert, minimum, maximum and split-point moves one level down and does O(1) work, so each is \u003cb>O(h)\u003c/b>. The range query is \u003cb>O(h + k)\u003c/b> for k reported keys.\u003c/p>\u003cp>h is between log2(n + 1) (a perfectly balanced tree) and n (a chain). Inserting keys in random order gives an expected depth of about 1.39 log2 n per search, but inserting them in sorted order gives a chain, where every operation is O(n). The \"why balance matters\" card measures this; for now remember that \u003cb>O(h) is only O(log n) if something keeps the tree balanced\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Space: the iterative search uses O(1) extra space; the recursive insert uses O(h) stack.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A BST is the right model whenever a problem gives you one, or needs order plus updates: \"next larger key\", floor and ceiling, sorted iteration while keys change, range counts. Compared with the alternatives:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Structure\u003c/th>\u003cth>Search\u003c/th>\u003cth>Insert/delete\u003c/th>\u003cth>Next larger key, range scan\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hash set or dict\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) average\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) average\u003c/td>\u003ctd>not supported (no order)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the default when order does not matter\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted list + bisect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) (shifting)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n + k)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>excellent when updates are rare\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Balanced BST\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n + k)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>what std::map and TreeMap provide\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Plain BST\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h + k)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>fine for random keys, terrible for sorted ones\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In Python you rarely hand-write a BST for production code (the ordered-maps card explains what to use instead), but interview problems hand you BSTs constantly, and the invariant-driven walk is the core of every ordered structure, including B-trees.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Insert 35 into the tree above, then search for 55:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">insert 35: compare with the root. 35 &lt; 40, so 35 belongs in the left subtree\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  50   70\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">35 &gt; 20: go right. Everything in 20's left subtree is smaller than 20, so it is ruled out\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       40\n     /    \\\n   \u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  50   70\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">35 &gt; 30: go right. 30 has no right child, so the search falls off the tree here\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       40\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark>  50   70\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Attach 35 as the right child of 30. Three comparisons, one per level, and the invariant still holds\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       40\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  50   70\n       \\\n        \u003cmark>35\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">search 55: 55 &gt; 40 go right, 55 &lt; 60 go left, 55 &gt; 50 go right: None. Absent, and that None is where 55 would go\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       40\n     /    \\\n   20      \u003cmark>60\u003c/mark>\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>   70\n       \\\n        35\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = left\n        self.right = right\n\n\ndef search(node, key):\n    \"\"\"Node holding key, or None. Iterative: O(h) time, O(1) space.\"\"\"\n    while node is not None and node.val != key:\n        node = node.left if key &lt; node.val else node.right\n    return node\n\n\ndef insert(node, key):\n    \"\"\"Insert key (duplicates are ignored) and return the subtree's root.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return TreeNode(key)\n    if key &lt; node.val:\n        node.left = insert(node.left, key)\n    elif key &gt; node.val:\n        node.right = insert(node.right, key)\n    return node\n\n\ndef minimum(node):\n    \"\"\"Leftmost node of a non-empty subtree.\"\"\"\n    while node.left is not None:\n        node = node.left\n    return node\n\n\ndef split_point(root, p, q):\n    \"\"\"Lowest common ancestor of keys p and q, both present: where their paths part.\"\"\"\n    node = root\n    while node:\n        if p &lt; node.val and q &lt; node.val:\n            node = node.left\n        elif p &gt; node.val and q &gt; node.val:\n            node = node.right\n        else:\n            return node\n\n\ndef range_sum(node, lo, hi):\n    \"\"\"Sum of keys in [lo, hi], skipping subtrees that cannot contain any.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    if node.val &lt; lo:\n        return range_sum(node.right, lo, hi)\n    if node.val &gt; hi:\n        return range_sum(node.left, lo, hi)\n    return node.val + range_sum(node.left, lo, hi) + range_sum(node.right, lo, hi)\n\n\nroot = None\nfor key in [40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70, 35]:\n    root = insert(root, key)\nprint(search(root, 35).val, search(root, 55))   # 35 None\nprint(minimum(root).val, split_point(root, 10, 35).val, split_point(root, 35, 50).val)   # 10 20 40\nprint(range_sum(root, 25, 55))   # 155\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>TreeNode\u003c/code> is the same class as in the binary trees module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing only with the parent\u003c/b> when reasoning about where a key can be: the invariant is about whole subtrees, and code that ignores that (for example in validation, two cards ahead) is wrong.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting to reassign the child pointer\u003c/b>: calling \u003ccode>insert(node.left, key)\u003c/code> without \u003ccode>node.left = ...\u003c/code> loses the new node whenever the child was None.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Duplicates\u003c/b>: decide a policy (ignore, count, or always go right) and apply it everywhere, or search and delete will disagree with insert.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursion on degenerate trees\u003c/b>: a recursive insert on a 10^4-node chain exceeds Python's recursion limit. Use the iterative walk (shown in the balance card) when the tree can be skewed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Range pruning the wrong way round\u003c/b>: when \u003ccode>node.val &lt; lo\u003c/code>, the \u003ci>left\u003c/i> subtree is useless, not the right one.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the trace tree (with 35 inserted), which nodes does \u003ccode>range_sum(root, 25, 55)\u003c/code> visit, and what does it return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>40 is in range (add 40, visit both subtrees). 20 &lt; 25, so only its right subtree is visited: 30 is in range (add 30, visit both children: None on the left, 35 on the right, add 35). On the right of the root, 60 &gt; 55, so only its left subtree: 50 is in range (add 50). 10 and 70 are never visited. The sum is 40 + 30 + 35 + 50 = 155.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is it safe for search to discard the whole right subtree when the key is smaller than the node's key?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The invariant says every key in the right subtree is greater than the node's key, and the key we want is smaller than the node's key, so it is smaller than every key there. It cannot be in that subtree, so ignoring it loses nothing. Applying this at every step proves that if the key exists, the walk reaches it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You must support \"insert a score\" and \"what is the smallest score greater than x?\" for 10^5 operations. Hash set, sorted list with bisect, or a balanced search tree?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A hash set cannot answer \"smallest greater than x\" without scanning. A sorted list answers it in O(log n) but each insert shifts O(n) elements, about 5 * 10^9 element moves in the worst case for 10^5 inserts, though in Python's C memmove that is often still a few seconds. A balanced tree (or, in Python, a SortedList) does both in O(log n), which is the right choice.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Where does the split point of keys 10 and 30 lie in the trace tree, and why is a node higher up not their lowest common ancestor?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At 20: from the root both are smaller than 40, so both go left; at 20, 10 goes left and 30 goes right, so their paths part there. 40 is a common ancestor, but not the lowest, because 20 is also a common ancestor and is deeper.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>700\u003c/b>: iterative search. Expected: on the tree built from \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70]\u003c/code>, searching 30 returns the subtree rooted at 30 (level order \u003ccode>[30]\u003c/code>), searching 60 returns \u003ccode>[60, 50, 70]\u003c/code>, and searching 55 returns None; O(h) time, O(1) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>235\u003c/b>: split-point lowest common ancestor. Expected on the same tree: (10, 30) gives 20, (10, 70) gives 40, (50, 60) gives 60 (a node can be its own ancestor); O(h) time, O(1) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>938\u003c/b>: range sum with pruning. Expected on the same tree: [25, 55] gives 120 (30 + 40 + 50), [10, 70] gives 280, [61, 69] gives 0; O(h + k) time. Count the nodes visited for [61, 69] (expected: 40, 60 and 70, then one empty child).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how a binary search tree turns every lookup, insert, minimum, lowest common ancestor and range query into a walk down one path guided by its ordering invariant.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:bst/how-do-you-delete-a-key-from-a-binary-search-tre":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find the node, then: a \u003cb>leaf\u003c/b> simply disappears; a node with \u003cb>one child\u003c/b> is replaced by that child; a node with \u003cb>two children\u003c/b> takes the key of its \u003cb>in-order successor\u003c/b> (the smallest key in its right subtree), and that successor, which has no left child, is then deleted from the right subtree by one of the easy cases. Write it as \"return the new root of this subtree\" and every case is a one-line return.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Think of a tournament bracket or a filing cabinet with a strict order. Removing a folder at the end of a shelf is trivial. Removing one with a single neighbour group behind it means sliding that group into its place. Removing one in the middle of a full shelf needs a replacement that fits exactly between the left and right neighbours: the next folder in order is the natural choice, because everything left of the gap is smaller than it and everything right of it is larger.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Step 1: find it, returning subtree roots\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>delete(node, key)\u003c/code> returns the root of the subtree after deletion. If the key is smaller, recurse left and reassign \u003ccode>node.left\u003c/code>; if larger, recurse right; if the subtree is empty, the key is absent and you return None. This is the same shape as insert.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Step 2: the three cases at the node holding the key\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>No children\u003c/b>: return None. The parent's pointer becomes None.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One child\u003c/b>: return that child. The whole child subtree moves up one level. Order is preserved because that subtree was already on the correct side of every ancestor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two children\u003c/b>: the replacement must be larger than everything on the left and smaller than everything on the right. Two keys qualify: the \u003cb>predecessor\u003c/b> (maximum of the left subtree) and the \u003cb>successor\u003c/b> (minimum of the right subtree). Take the successor: walk right once, then left until you cannot. Copy its key into the node, then delete the successor's key from the right subtree. The successor has no left child (otherwise that child would be smaller), so that second deletion is case 1 or 2.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch5>Why the order survives\u003c/h5>\u003cp>After case 3 the node holds s, the minimum of the old right subtree. Every key on the left was smaller than the deleted key, which was smaller than s: fine. Every remaining key on the right was larger than s, since s was the minimum there: fine. Every ancestor's constraint on this subtree was satisfied by s before, because s was already in this subtree. So the invariant holds everywhere.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Copying keys versus relinking nodes\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Copying \u003ccode>succ.val\u003c/code> into the node is what interview solutions do. Libraries usually \u003cb>relink\u003c/b> the successor node into the deleted node's position instead, because callers may hold references or iterators to nodes and expect a node's key never to change, and because a node may carry a large value payload. The structure of the argument is identical.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Trimming to a range\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Trimming a tree so that only keys in [lo, hi] remain is deletion in bulk with the range-pruning logic from the previous card: a node below lo is dropped together with its whole left subtree, and the trimmed right subtree takes its place; symmetrically above hi; a node in range keeps itself and trims both children.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Finding the node is O(h); finding the successor walks at most another O(h) down the same side; the second deletion is O(1) once there. So deletion is \u003cb>O(h) time\u003c/b> and O(h) stack for the recursive version. Trimming visits every kept node plus the boundary paths: O(n) in the worst case, which is unavoidable when many nodes are kept, since each needs its children rechecked.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Deletion does not rebalance. Always taking the successor is also asymmetric: algs4 shows a 150-key tree, under long random sequences of inserts and successor-only (Hibbard) deletions, becoming visibly skewed to the left. Alternating between successor and predecessor reduces the drift, but only a self-balancing tree guarantees the height, one more reason production code uses them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Problems that ask you to delete, trim or prune a BST in place and return the root.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Any ordered-set implementation; in Python you would normally call \u003ccode>SortedList.remove\u003c/code> instead of writing this.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Lazy deletion (marking a node as deleted and skipping it) is an alternative when deletions are rare or must be cheap, at the cost of memory and longer paths; heaps use the same trick.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Delete 40 (two children) from this tree, \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70, None, None, None, None, None, 55]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">delete(root, 40): the key is at the root, and the root has two children, so it needs a replacement\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  50   70\n           \\\n            55\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Find the in-order successor: step right to 60, then left while possible: 50. 50 has no left child, so 50 is the successor\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       40\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>   70\n           \\\n            55\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Copy 50 into the root. For a moment 50 appears twice; the tree is not valid yet\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  50   70\n           \\\n            55\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">delete(right subtree, 50): 50 &lt; 60, go left; the node 50 has only a right child, so the call returns 55 (case 2)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       50\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>   70\n           \\\n            \u003cmark>55\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">60.left = 55. The in-order sequence is 10 20 30 50 55 60 70: sorted, with 40 gone\u003c/p>\u003cpre>       50\n     /    \\\n   20      60\n  /  \\    /  \\\n10   30  \u003cmark>55\u003c/mark>   70\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def delete(node, key):\n    \"\"\"Remove key if present and return the subtree's new root.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return None\n    if key &lt; node.val:\n        node.left = delete(node.left, key)\n    elif key &gt; node.val:\n        node.right = delete(node.right, key)\n    else:\n        if node.left is None:               # no child, or only a right child\n            return node.right\n        if node.right is None:              # only a left child\n            return node.left\n        succ = node.right                   # two children: in-order successor\n        while succ.left is not None:\n            succ = succ.left\n        node.val = succ.val                 # copy it up ...\n        node.right = delete(node.right, succ.val)   # ... and remove it below\n    return node\n\n\ndef trim(node, lo, hi):\n    \"\"\"Keep only keys in [lo, hi] and return the new root; the result is still a BST.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return None\n    if node.val &lt; lo:                       # node and its left subtree are too small\n        return trim(node.right, lo, hi)\n    if node.val &gt; hi:                       # node and its right subtree are too large\n        return trim(node.left, lo, hi)\n    node.left = trim(node.left, lo, hi)\n    node.right = trim(node.right, lo, hi)\n    return node\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The first \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> in the found branch covers both the leaf (returns None) and the right-child-only case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the second deletion\u003c/b>: copying the successor's key up without deleting it from the right subtree leaves a duplicate key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deleting the successor by value in the wrong subtree\u003c/b>: it must be deleted from \u003ccode>node.right\u003c/code>, starting at the right child; starting at \u003ccode>node\u003c/code> would find the copied key at the node itself and loop on the two-child case.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Not reassigning child pointers\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>delete(node.left, key)\u003c/code> without \u003ccode>node.left = ...\u003c/code> has no effect when the deleted node was the child itself.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Losing the root\u003c/b>: always use the return value, \u003ccode>root = delete(root, key)\u003c/code>, because the root itself may be deleted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Successor without a right subtree\u003c/b>: the \"right once, then left\" walk only works when the right child exists, which is guaranteed in the two-child case. (A general successor query for any node is different; see the in-order card.)\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Starting again from the trace's original tree, delete 20. What is the successor, and what does the tree look like afterwards?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>20 has two children (10 and 30). Its successor is the minimum of its right subtree, which is 30 itself (30 has no left child). Copy 30 into the node, then delete 30 from the right subtree: 30 is a leaf, so it disappears. The left subtree of 40 becomes 30 with left child 10 and no right child.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the successor never have a left child, and why does that matter?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The successor is the minimum of the right subtree, found by going left until there is no left child; if it had a left child, that child would be smaller and would be the minimum instead. It matters because it guarantees the second deletion is a no-child or one-child case, so the recursion does not need the two-child case again and stays O(h).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For [lo, hi] = [25, 55], trim the trace's original tree. Which nodes survive, and what is the new root?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>40 is in range and stays as the root. On the left, 20 &lt; 25, so 20 and 10 go and 20's trimmed right subtree, 30, takes its place. On the right, 60 &gt; 55, so 60 and 70 go and 60's trimmed left subtree replaces it: 50, which is in range, with its right child 55 kept. Result: 40 with left child 30 and right child 50, and 55 to the right of 50.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's two-child case is \u003ccode>node.val = succ.val; node.right = delete(node, succ.val)\u003c/code>. What happens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The call starts at \u003ccode>node\u003c/code>, whose key now equals \u003ccode>succ.val\u003c/code>, so it lands in the \"found\" branch at the same node again, finds the same two children and the same successor, and recurses forever (until RecursionError). The second deletion must start at \u003ccode>node.right\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>450\u003c/b>: deletion with the three cases. Expected: deleting 40 from \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70, None, None, None, None, None, 55]\u003c/code> gives in-order \u003ccode>[10, 20, 30, 50, 55, 60, 70]\u003c/code> with root 50; deleting a key that is absent returns the tree unchanged; deleting the only node returns None. O(h) time. Stress-test it: apply random inserts and deletes and compare the in-order list with a Python \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> sorted after each step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>669\u003c/b>: trim to a range. Expected: trimming the same tree to [25, 55] gives level order \u003ccode>[40, 30, 50, None, None, None, 55]\u003c/code>; trimming to [61, 69] gives an empty tree; O(n) time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to delete from a binary search tree in all three cases, why replacing with the in-order successor keeps the order, and how to trim a tree to a range.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:bst/how-do-you-check-that-a-binary-tree-really-is-a-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Being a BST is a property of \u003cb>whole subtrees\u003c/b>: a node must be greater than \u003ci>every\u003c/i> key on its left and smaller than \u003ci>every\u003c/i> key on its right, so each node is constrained by all its ancestors, not just its parent. Pass the allowed open interval (low, high) down the tree, narrowing it at each step: going left sets high to the parent's key, going right sets low. Equivalent checks: the in-order sequence is strictly increasing, or each subtree reports its (min, max) upward.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A new employee is told \"your salary must be below your manager's\". That is a local rule, and it is not enough: a company also expects everyone in a department to earn less than its head, and everyone in the sister department to earn more than whatever separates the two. A rule from two levels up still binds you. The bounds are those inherited rules: each time you step down, you inherit your parent's constraints and add one new one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Why children-only checks fail\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          10\n         /  \\\n        5    15\n            /  \\\n           6    20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every node is consistent with its children: 5 &lt; 10 &lt; 15, and 6 &lt; 15 &lt; 20. But 6 sits in the right subtree of 10 and is smaller than 10, so a search for 6 would go left from the root and never find it. The tree is not a BST.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Bounds passed down\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ok(node, low, high)\u003c/code> asks: are all keys in this subtree strictly between low and high, and is the subtree a BST? The root gets (-inf, inf). A node fails if its key is outside its interval; otherwise its left child gets (low, node.val) and its right child gets (node.val, high). By induction, every key ends up checked against the tightest bound from each ancestor, which is exactly the invariant. This is the \"pass information down\" pattern from the binary trees module.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>In-order must be strictly increasing\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The in-order sequence of a BST is sorted (the next card proves it), and conversely a binary tree whose in-order sequence is strictly increasing is a BST. So walk in-order, remember the previous key, and fail on the first key that is not larger. The iterative walk can stop at the first violation.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Summaries passed up\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Each subtree can report (is_bst, min, max, sum). A node is the root of a BST exactly when both children are, \u003ccode>left.max &lt; node.val &lt; right.min\u003c/code>, with an empty child reporting (True, +inf, -inf, 0) so that it never blocks the comparison. This version answers richer questions in the same pass, such as \"the largest key sum of any subtree that is a BST\", where the bounds-down version cannot, because a subtree that fails inside a larger tree may still be a valid BST on its own.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Equal keys and sentinel values\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The definitions above are strict, so duplicates make the tree invalid; if your BST allows duplicates on one side, make that comparison non-strict. In Python, \u003ccode>float(\"-inf\")\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>float(\"inf\")\u003c/code> compare with any int. In Java or C++, do not use \u003ccode>Integer.MIN_VALUE\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>INT_MAX\u003c/code> as sentinels when keys can equal them; use nullable bounds or a 64-bit type.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All three versions visit each node once with O(1) work: \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b>. Space is O(h) for the recursion or the in-order stack. The children-only check is also O(n), which is why it is tempting, but it answers a different question.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A brute force that checks every node against every key in both of its subtrees is O(n h): O(n log n) balanced, O(n^2) on a chain. It is still worth writing as a stress-test oracle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Approach\u003c/th>\u003cth>Best when\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strength\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Bounds passed down\u003c/td>\u003ctd>you only need yes or no\u003c/td>\u003ctd>stops at the first violation, simple proof\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>In-order strictly increasing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>you are already walking in order (iterators, recovery)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>reuses the iterative in-order loop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Summaries passed up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>you need facts about every subtree (largest BST subtree, sums)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>answers per-subtree questions in one pass\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The bounds pattern also builds trees: to rebuild a BST from its pre-order list in O(n), create nodes while the next value fits the current (low, high) window.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>ok(node, low, high)\u003c/code> on the counterexample tree above:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">ok(10, -inf, inf): 10 is inside (-inf, inf). The left child gets (-inf, 10) and the right child gets (10, inf)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node 10   bounds (-inf, inf)    \u003cmark>ok\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">ok(5, -inf, 10): 5 is inside. It is a leaf, so its empty children return True: the left subtree is valid\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node 5    bounds (-inf, 10)     \u003cmark>ok\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">ok(15, 10, inf): 15 is inside. Its left child gets (10, 15), its right child (15, inf)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node 15   bounds (10, inf)      \u003cmark>ok\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">ok(6, 10, 15): 6 is NOT greater than 10. The bound came from the root, two levels up, which a children-only check never sees\u003c/p>\u003cpre>node 6    bounds \u003cmark>(10, 15)\u003c/mark>       \u003cmark>fails\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The False propagates up through 15 and 10 (the and short-circuits, so 20 is never checked). The tree is not a BST\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result    \u003cmark>False\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def is_bst(root):\n    \"\"\"Strict check: every key lies strictly inside the bounds set by its ancestors.\"\"\"\n    def ok(node, low, high):\n        if node is None:\n            return True\n        if not low &lt; node.val &lt; high:\n            return False\n        return ok(node.left, low, node.val) and ok(node.right, node.val, high)\n\n    return ok(root, float(\"-inf\"), float(\"inf\"))\n\n\ndef is_bst_inorder(root):\n    \"\"\"The same check via an iterative in-order walk: keys must strictly increase.\"\"\"\n    prev, stack, node = None, [], root\n    while stack or node:\n        while node:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        if prev is not None and node.val &lt;= prev:\n            return False\n        prev = node.val\n        node = node.right\n    return True\n\n\ndef max_sum_bst(root):\n    \"\"\"Largest key sum of any subtree that is a BST (0 for the empty subtree).\"\"\"\n    best = 0\n\n    def walk(node):\n        \"\"\"(is_bst, smallest key, largest key, key sum) of the subtree.\"\"\"\n        nonlocal best\n        if node is None:\n            return True, float(\"inf\"), float(\"-inf\"), 0\n        l_ok, l_min, l_max, l_sum = walk(node.left)\n        r_ok, r_min, r_max, r_sum = walk(node.right)\n        if l_ok and r_ok and l_max &lt; node.val &lt; r_min:\n            total = l_sum + node.val + r_sum\n            best = max(best, total)\n            return True, min(l_min, node.val), max(r_max, node.val), total\n        return False, 0, 0, 0                # the other fields are never read\n\n    walk(root)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Children-only comparison\u003c/b>: passes every test whose violations are one level deep and fails on the ones that matter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Non-strict comparisons by accident\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>low &lt;= node.val\u003c/code> accepts duplicates; decide on purpose.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflowing sentinels\u003c/b> in fixed-width languages, as described above.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursion depth\u003c/b>: a valid but skewed BST of 10^4 nodes is 10^4 deep; the iterative in-order version avoids the limit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Empty child summaries\u003c/b>: if an empty child returned (True, 0, 0, 0), a node with value -5 and no left child would fail \u003ccode>0 &lt; -5\u003c/code>. The infinities make empty children neutral.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run \u003ccode>is_bst\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>[8, 3, 12, 1, 9]\u003c/code>. Which call fails, and with which bounds?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The root 8 gets (-inf, inf); 3 gets (-inf, 8); 1 gets (-inf, 3) and passes; 9, the right child of 3, gets (3, 8) and fails because 9 &gt; 8. It is larger than its parent, as a right child should be, but it is inside the left subtree of 8.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does checking that the in-order sequence strictly increases prove that the tree is a BST?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Take any node x. Its left subtree's keys appear immediately before x in the in-order sequence and its right subtree's keys immediately after it. If the whole sequence is strictly increasing, every left-subtree key is smaller than x and every right-subtree key is larger, which is the BST invariant at x. This holds for every x.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">To find the largest-sum subtree that is a BST, why can you not reuse \u003ccode>is_bst\u003c/code> on every node?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You could, but it would be O(n^2) on a chain (a full check per node), and the bounds version only says yes or no for the whole tree, not for each subtree. Returning (is_bst, min, max, sum) upward answers the question for every subtree in one O(n) pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A Java solution uses \u003ccode>Integer.MIN_VALUE\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Integer.MAX_VALUE\u003c/code> as initial bounds with strict comparisons. Which single-node input breaks it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A tree whose only key is \u003ccode>Integer.MAX_VALUE\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>Integer.MIN_VALUE\u003c/code>): the check \u003ccode>MIN &lt; val &lt; MAX\u003c/code> is false, so a valid one-node BST is rejected. Use \u003ccode>Long\u003c/code> bounds or null for \"no bound\".\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>98\u003c/b>: bounds passed down. Expected: True for \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70]\u003c/code>, False for \u003ccode>[10, 5, 15, None, None, 6, 20]\u003c/code> and for \u003ccode>[8, 3, 12, 1, 9]\u003c/code>, False for \u003ccode>[2, 2, 2]\u003c/code> (strict), True for an empty tree; O(n) time. Check it against \u003ccode>is_bst_inorder\u003c/code> and a brute force on random trees.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1373\u003c/b>: summaries passed up. Expected: 24 for \u003ccode>[5, 2, 8, 1, 9, 6, 10]\u003c/code> (the whole tree fails because 9 sits left of 5, so the best is the subtree 8, 6, 10), 0 for \u003ccode>[-4, -6, -2]\u003c/code> (a valid BST with a negative sum loses to the empty subtree), and the full sum 280 for \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70]\u003c/code>; O(n) time. Compare with a brute force that validates and sums every subtree separately.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why a binary search tree must be validated against bounds from every ancestor, and three O(n) ways to check it: bounds down, in-order, and summaries up.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:bst/why-does-in-order-traversal-of-a-bst-come-out-so":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In a BST, in-order (left, node, right) visits keys in \u003cb>increasing order\u003c/b>, so every \"sorted\" question becomes a walk. Stop an iterative in-order after k pops for the \u003cb>k-th smallest\u003c/b>; walk down from the root remembering the last left turn for the \u003cb>successor\u003c/b>; keep the in-order stack in an object for an \u003cb>iterator\u003c/b> with O(1) amortised \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> and O(h) memory; and look for \u003cb>descents\u003c/b> in the in-order sequence to find keys that break the order.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A BST is a sorted list folded into a tree: the root is somewhere in the middle, everything smaller hangs to the left, everything larger to the right. Unfolding it means reading the left part completely, then the root, then the right part, at every level. That is exactly in-order. You can see it by squashing the tree flat: each key drops straight down onto a number line in sorted position.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>           40\n         /    \\\n       20      60\n      /  \\    /  \\\n    10   30  50   70\n    |    |   |    |\n    10 20 30 40 50 60 70     in-order: the keys in sorted order\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The proof\u003c/h5>\u003cp>By induction on the size of the tree. An empty tree gives the empty sequence, which is sorted. For a node x, in-order is \u003ccode>in(x.left) + [x] + in(x.right)\u003c/code>. By the induction hypothesis both parts are sorted; by the invariant every key on the left is smaller than x and every key on the right is larger. So the concatenation is sorted.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>k-th smallest: stop early\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Run the iterative in-order from the binary trees module and count pops; the k-th pop is the answer. The walk first descends the left spine (O(h)), then each pop is the next key, so it costs \u003cb>O(h + k)\u003c/b>, not O(n). If you need many k-th queries on a changing tree, store the size of every subtree in its node (an \u003cb>augmented\u003c/b> tree): at a node with left-subtree size L, the answer is the node if k = L + 1, in the left subtree if k &lt;= L, and the (k - L - 1)-th of the right subtree otherwise. That is O(h) per query and is how order-statistic trees, and SortedList's positional index, answer \u003ccode>s[k]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Successor, predecessor, floor and ceiling\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The \u003cb>successor\u003c/b> of a key is the smallest key larger than it. From the root: if the node's key is larger than the target, it is a candidate, and a smaller candidate can only be in its left subtree, so record it and go left; otherwise go right. The last candidate recorded is the answer. The \u003cb>ceiling\u003c/b> (smallest key &gt;= x) uses \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code>; the \u003cb>predecessor\u003c/b> and \u003cb>floor\u003c/b> mirror both. Each is one path: O(h). With parent pointers, the successor of a given node is the leftmost node of its right subtree if it has one, otherwise its first ancestor that it reaches from the left.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>An iterator that pauses\u003c/h5>\u003cp>An iterator must return the next key on each call without walking the whole tree up front. Keep the in-order stack as state: the constructor pushes the left spine from the root; \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> pops a node, pushes the left spine of its right child, and returns the popped key. Memory is the stack, O(h). Over a full iteration each node is pushed once and popped once, so n calls cost O(n) in total: \u003cb>O(1) amortised\u003c/b> per call, even though a single call can push O(h) nodes. This is the pattern behind iterators in std::map, TreeMap and database cursors.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Repairing two swapped keys\u003c/h5>\u003cp>If two keys of a BST were swapped by mistake, the in-order sequence is sorted except for those two. Swapping neighbours creates one \u003cb>descent\u003c/b> (a place where a key is followed by a smaller one); swapping distant keys creates two. The first wrong key is the larger key of the first descent, and the second is the smaller key of the last descent. Find them in one in-order pass and swap their values back.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time\u003c/th>\u003cth>Extra space\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>full in-order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>k-th smallest by early stopping\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h + k)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>k-th smallest with subtree sizes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) per node, kept up to date on insert and delete\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>successor, floor, ceiling from the root\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>iterator, n calls to next\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) total, O(1) amortised each\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>recover two swapped keys\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(h) (O(1) with Morris traversal)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Any question about a BST that mentions order: the smallest, the k-th, the next, ranks, adjacent differences, merging two BSTs, or \"visit keys from largest to smallest\" (reverse in-order: right, node, left, which also solves \"replace each key by the sum of all keys greater than or equal to it\"). If you only need the k-th smallest of a fixed array, quickselect or a heap is cheaper than building a tree; if the set changes and k-th queries are frequent, an augmented or library structure is worth it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>BSTIterator\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70]\u003c/code>, calling \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> until the stack is empty:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Constructor: push the left spine from the root: 40, 20, 10\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [40, 20, \u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>]\noutput  []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next(): pop 10 and return it. 10 has no right child, so nothing is pushed\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [40, 20]\noutput  [\u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next(): pop 20. Push the left spine of its right child: just 30\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [40, \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark>]\noutput  [10, \u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next(): pop 30 (no right child)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [40]\noutput  [10, 20, \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next(): pop 40. Push the left spine of 60: 60, then 50. This call did the most work: two pushes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [\u003cmark>60\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>]\noutput  [10, 20, 30, \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next() three more times: pop 50; pop 60 and push 70; pop 70. The stack is empty, so has_next() is False\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   []\noutput  [10, 20, 30, 40, \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>60\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>70\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Seven pushes and seven pops for seven keys: O(1) amortised per call, and the stack never held more than 3 nodes, the height.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def kth_smallest(root, k):\n    \"\"\"k-th smallest key (1-based), by stopping the in-order walk after k pops.\"\"\"\n    stack, node = [], root\n    while stack or node:\n        while node:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        k -= 1\n        if k == 0:\n            return node.val\n        node = node.right\n    raise ValueError(\"k is larger than the number of keys\")\n\n\ndef successor(root, key):\n    \"\"\"Smallest key strictly greater than key, or None. O(h), no parent pointers.\"\"\"\n    best, node = None, root\n    while node:\n        if node.val &gt; key:\n            best = node.val          # a candidate; a smaller one can only be on the left\n            node = node.left\n        else:\n            node = node.right\n    return best\n\n\nclass BSTIterator:\n    \"\"\"Keys in increasing order; next() is O(1) amortised and memory is O(h).\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, root):\n        self.stack = []\n        self._push_left(root)\n\n    def _push_left(self, node):\n        while node:\n            self.stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n\n    def has_next(self):\n        return bool(self.stack)\n\n    def next(self):\n        node = self.stack.pop()\n        self._push_left(node.right)\n        return node.val\n\n\ndef recover(root):\n    \"\"\"Two keys were swapped: find them in one in-order pass and swap them back.\"\"\"\n    first = second = prev = None\n    stack, node = [], root\n    while stack or node:\n        while node:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        if prev is not None and prev.val &gt; node.val:    # a descent\n            if first is None:\n                first = prev                             # larger key of the first descent\n            second = node                                # smaller key of the latest descent\n        prev = node\n        node = node.right\n    first.val, second.val = second.val, first.val\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>LeetCode's version of the iterator names the methods \u003ccode>hasNext\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>next\u003c/code>; the logic is the same.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Collecting everything first\u003c/b>: building the full in-order list and indexing \u003ccode>[k - 1]\u003c/code> is O(n) time and memory, and it does not answer the iterator problem's O(h) memory requirement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off by one in k\u003c/b>: k is 1-based in most statements; decrement before comparing, as in the template.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Successor with \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: that is the ceiling, which returns the key itself when present. Read whether \"next\" means strictly larger.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recovering with only one descent\u003c/b>: when the swapped keys are adjacent in order, there is only one descent, and both nodes come from it; setting \u003ccode>second\u003c/code> at every descent handles both cases.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Changing the tree under an iterator\u003c/b>: inserting or deleting while an iterator holds a stack can make it skip or repeat keys, the same reason Python forbids changing a dict's size during iteration.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the iterator trace, how many nodes are on the stack right after \u003ccode>next()\u003c/code> returns 40, and what will the next two calls return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two: 60 and 50 (50 on top). The next call pops 50 and returns it (no right child); the one after pops 60, pushes 70 and returns 60.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is \u003ccode>next()\u003c/code> O(1) amortised even though one call can push h nodes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every node is pushed exactly once (when the walk first reaches it on some left spine) and popped exactly once (when it is returned). Over a full iteration of n calls that is n pushes and n pops in total, O(n), so the average per call is O(1), however unevenly the pushes are spread.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The in-order sequence of a BST with two swapped keys is \u003ccode>[10, 20, 60, 40, 50, 30, 70]\u003c/code>. Which keys were swapped, and how does the algorithm find them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>30 and 60. There are two descents: 60 &gt; 40 and 50 &gt; 30. The first wrong key is the larger element of the first descent (60); the second is the smaller element of the last descent (30). Swapping them back gives 10 20 30 40 50 60 70.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need the k-th smallest key 10^5 times while keys are also being inserted and deleted. What do you change, and what does each query then cost?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Store in every node the size of its subtree and update it along the path on each insert and delete. A query then walks one path, comparing k with the left subtree's size at each node: O(h) per query, O(log n) if the tree is balanced. In Python, \u003ccode>SortedList\u003c/code> gives the same with \u003ccode>s[k - 1]\u003c/code> in about O(log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>230\u003c/b>: k-th smallest by early stopping. Expected on \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70]\u003c/code>: k = 1 gives 10, k = 4 gives 40, k = 7 gives 70; O(h + k) time, O(h) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>173\u003c/b>: the iterator. Expected: on the same tree the calls return 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70 and then \u003ccode>hasNext\u003c/code> is False; the stack never exceeds the height (3 here). Check with a counter that the total number of pushes equals the number of keys.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>99\u003c/b>: recover two swapped keys. Expected: swapping 30 and 60 in the same tree (giving in-order \u003ccode>[10, 20, 60, 40, 50, 30, 70]\u003c/code>) is repaired to in-order \u003ccode>[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70]\u003c/code>; swapping the adjacent keys 40 and 50 (one descent) is repaired as well; O(n) time, O(h) space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why in-order traversal of a binary search tree is sorted and how to use it for k-th smallest, successors, iterators and repairing swapped keys.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:bst/why-does-the-shape-of-a-bst-decide-its-speed-and":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every BST operation costs O(h), and h depends only on the \u003cb>insertion order\u003c/b>. Random order gives a height of a small multiple of log2 n (measured: about 47 for a million keys, against 20 for a perfect tree), but sorted or nearly sorted input gives a \u003cb>chain of height n\u003c/b>, turning every operation into a linear scan and n inserts into O(n^2). If you have the keys up front, build the tree from the sorted list by always choosing the \u003cb>middle\u003c/b> key as the root: height ceil(log2(n + 1)), in O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A BST is a binary search whose pivots were chosen by whoever inserted first. The first key inserted becomes the root forever (in a plain BST), so if it is the smallest key, the whole rest of the data goes to one side. Insert 1, 2, 3, ... and each new key is the largest so far: it walks to the bottom of the right spine and hangs off the end. You have built a linked list with extra pointers.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Real data loves to arrive sorted: timestamps, auto-increment ids, log lines, files that were sorted by a previous job. That is why the plain BST is a teaching structure, and why the next card exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Heights you should expect\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Insertion order\u003c/th>\u003cth>Height of n keys\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost of n inserts\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost of one search\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>sorted or reverse sorted\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about n^2 / 2 comparisons\u003c/td>\u003ctd>up to n\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>random\u003c/td>\u003ctd>measured below: about 2 to 2.5 times log2 n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n) expected\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 1.39 log2 n on average\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>built from sorted keys, middle first\u003c/td>\u003ctd>ceil(log2(n + 1))\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) to build\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most log2 n + 1\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Measured with an iterative insert in CPython (mean over five random orders, one for a million):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>n\u003c/th>\u003cth>log2 n\u003c/th>\u003cth>height, random order\u003c/th>\u003cth>height, sorted order\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>22\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>13.3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>32\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>100,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>16.6\u003c/td>\u003ctd>41\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1,000,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>19.9\u003c/td>\u003ctd>47\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,000,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The average search depth of a random BST is about 2 ln n, roughly 1.39 log2 n, the same as the number of comparisons quicksort makes per element with random pivots: the root of a random BST is a random pivot, and each subtree is the same story recursively. The height (the worst node) is larger, but still logarithmic.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Building a balanced tree from sorted keys\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Pick the middle key as the root, then build the left half and the right half recursively. Each subtree gets a contiguous slice of the sorted keys, so the result is a BST, and sizes of the two halves differ by at most one at every node, so the height is the minimum possible. Use indices, not slices, to keep it O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp>The same idea \u003cb>rebalances\u003c/b> any BST: read its keys in-order into a list (O(n)), then rebuild from the middle. Scapegoat trees do exactly that, rebuilding a subtree only when it becomes too lopsided, which keeps the height logarithmic with amortised O(log n) updates and no rotations at all.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Dealing with unknown order\u003c/h5>\u003cp>If you cannot control the order, you have three options: shuffle the keys first if you have them all (random order, expected logarithmic height), use a self-balancing tree (next card), or use a structure that does not care about order at all (a sorted array with bisect for mostly static data, SortedList in Python).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inserting the keys 1..n in order into a plain BST costs 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (n - 1) = n(n - 1)/2 comparisons: O(n^2). For n = 100,000 that is about 5 * 10^9 comparisons, minutes in CPython. Doubling n quadruples the time, which is the signature to look for in a benchmark.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The balanced build makes one node per key with O(1) work: \u003cb>O(n) time\u003c/b>, O(log n) recursion depth.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>You receive sorted data and need a tree (a sorted array or list \"converted to a BST\", a static index built once): build from the middle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A tree has become lopsided and you can afford one O(n) pass: in-order to a list, then rebuild.\u003c/li>\u003cli>You are benchmarking or testing any search tree: always include sorted, reverse-sorted and nearly-sorted inputs, not just random ones, because random data hides the worst case.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Insert the same five keys in two orders:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sorted order. Insert 10, then 20: 20 &gt; 10, so it becomes the right child. One comparison\u003c/p>\u003cpre>10\n  \\\n   \u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 30 and 40: each is larger than every key so far and walks the whole right spine: 2, then 3 comparisons\u003c/p>\u003cpre>10\n  \\\n   20\n     \\\n      30\n        \\\n         \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 50: 4 more comparisons. Height 5 for 5 keys: a linked list. Total 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10 comparisons, and a search for 50 visits every node\u003c/p>\u003cpre>10\n  \\\n   20\n     \\\n      30\n        \\\n         40\n           \\\n            \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Same keys, middle first: insert 30, then 10 and 40 (one comparison each)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>   \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark>\n  /    \\\n\u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>      \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 20 and 50 (two comparisons each). Height 3, total 0 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 6 comparisons. With n keys the gap grows to n^2/2 versus about n log2 n\u003c/p>\u003cpre>   30\n  /    \\\n10      40\n  \\       \\\n  \u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>      \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def build_balanced(keys):\n    \"\"\"Height-balanced BST from a sorted list of distinct keys: the middle key is the root.\"\"\"\n    def build(lo, hi):                       # the keys in keys[lo:hi]\n        if lo &gt;= hi:\n            return None\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        return TreeNode(keys[mid], build(lo, mid), build(mid + 1, hi))\n\n    return build(0, len(keys))\n\n\ndef insert_iterative(root, key):\n    \"\"\"Plain BST insert without recursion, safe on degenerate trees. Returns the root.\"\"\"\n    new = TreeNode(key)\n    if root is None:\n        return new\n    node = root\n    while True:\n        if key &lt; node.val:\n            if node.left is None:\n                node.left = new\n                return root\n            node = node.left\n        elif key &gt; node.val:\n            if node.right is None:\n                node.right = new\n                return root\n            node = node.right\n        else:\n            return root                      # duplicate: ignore\n\n\ndef height_iterative(root):\n    \"\"\"Number of levels, without recursion.\"\"\"\n    best, stack = 0, [(root, 1)] if root else []\n    while stack:\n        node, depth = stack.pop()\n        best = max(best, depth)\n        for child in (node.left, node.right):\n            if child:\n                stack.append((child, depth + 1))\n    return best\n\n\nchain = None\nfor key in range(1, 2001):\n    chain = insert_iterative(chain, key)\nprint(height_iterative(chain), height_iterative(build_balanced(list(range(1, 2001)))))   # 2000 11\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Testing only on random data\u003c/b>: the plain BST looks logarithmic until production feeds it sorted ids.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursive operations on the chain\u003c/b>: a recursive insert or traversal of a sorted-input tree with more than about 1000 keys raises RecursionError in Python before it even gets slow.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slicing in the balanced build\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>build(keys[:mid])\u003c/code> copies, making the build O(n log n) time and memory; pass indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unsorted input to the balanced build\u003c/b>: it silently produces a tree that is balanced but not a BST. Sort first, or assert sortedness in tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rebalancing too often\u003c/b>: an O(n) rebuild after every insert makes inserts O(n). Rebuild only when the height exceeds a threshold such as 2 log2 n, as scapegoat trees do.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>build_balanced([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\u003c/code> produce, in level order?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The middle index of 0..6 is 3, so the root is 4. The left part [1, 2, 3] has middle 2, with children 1 and 3. The right part [5, 6] has middle index (4 + 6) // 2 = 5, key 6, with left child 5. Level order: \u003ccode>[4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5]\u003c/code>, height 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is inserting n sorted keys into a plain BST Theta(n^2), not just O(n^2)?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The i-th key is larger than all i - 1 keys before it, which form a chain along the right spine, so its insert must compare with every one of them: exactly i - 1 comparisons. The total is n(n - 1)/2, so the bound is tight.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You must build a BST index once from 10^6 records that arrive sorted by key, then serve lookups only. Plain inserts, shuffled inserts, or the balanced build?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The balanced build: O(n) time and the minimum possible height (20 levels). Plain inserts would take about 5 * 10^11 comparisons and produce a chain; shuffled inserts would work (expected height around 47) but cost O(n log n) and give a taller tree. For lookups only, a sorted list with bisect is simpler still.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's benchmark shows the plain BST \"as fast as SortedList\" for 10^5 inserts. What should you ask about the input?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whether the keys were in random order. Random order gives a tree of height around 41 at that size, so the plain BST looks fine; on sorted or nearly sorted keys it degrades to a chain and needs about 5 * 10^9 comparisons. Ask for sorted, reverse-sorted and nearly-sorted runs too.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>108\u003c/b>: balanced build from a sorted array. Expected: \u003ccode>[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70]\u003c/code> gives level order \u003ccode>[40, 20, 60, 10, 30, 50, 70]\u003c/code> with the template's middle choice (other balanced answers are also accepted); \u003ccode>[1, 2]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[2, 1]\u003c/code>; the height for 2000 keys is 11; O(n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Degenerate versus balanced\u003c/b>: insert 1..2000 with \u003ccode>insert_iterative\u003c/code> and with the recursive insert from the first card. Expected: height 2000 for the iterative version, a \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> from the recursive one, and height 11 for \u003ccode>build_balanced(list(range(1, 2001)))\u003c/code>. Then time n = 1000, 2000, 4000, 8000 sorted inserts: each doubling should take about four times as long.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why insertion order decides a binary search tree's height, what heights to expect, and how to build or rebuild a perfectly balanced tree in O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:bst/how-do-avl-trees-red-black-trees-treaps-and-b-tr":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A self-balancing tree adds a second invariant that forces the height to be O(log n), and repairs it after every update with \u003cb>local\u003c/b> fixes that preserve the in-order sequence. AVL trees keep sibling subtree heights within 1 (height under 1.44 log2 n); red-black trees forbid two reds in a row and equalise black counts (height at most 2 log2(n + 1)); treaps make the shape random by heap-ordering random priorities (expected O(log n)); B-trees keep many keys per node and all leaves at the same depth (height about log base t of n). Binary trees repair with \u003cb>rotations\u003c/b>, B-trees with \u003cb>splits and merges\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A rotation is the BST version of changing which person in a line you call \"the middle\". Take a parent y with left child x. Making x the parent and y its right child changes the shape, but if you read the keys left to right they are still in the same order, because only who-sits-above-whom changed, not who-sits-left-of-whom:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          y                         x\n         / \\     rotate right      / \\\n        x   C    -----------&gt;     A   y\n       / \\       &lt;-----------        / \\\n      A   B      rotate left        B   C\n\n    in-order on both sides: A  x  B  y  C\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A, B and C are whole subtrees. Rotating right lifts A by one level and lowers C by one; B just changes parent. Three pointer assignments, O(1), and the BST invariant survives. Every balanced binary tree is \"a BST plus a rule about shape, plus rotations to restore the rule\".\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>AVL trees: heights within one\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003cb>Invariant\u003c/b>: at every node, the heights of the two subtrees differ by at most 1. Each node stores its height. After an insert or delete, walk back up the changed path updating heights; at the first node whose two heights differ by 2, one \u003cb>single\u003c/b> rotation (when the heavy child leans the same way) or one \u003cb>double\u003c/b> rotation (when it leans the other way: rotate the child first, then the node) restores the rule. After an insertion one such fix is enough; a deletion may need one per level.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why the height is logarithmic\u003c/b>: let N(h) be the fewest nodes an AVL tree of height h can have. Its two subtrees must have heights h - 1 and (at least) h - 2, so N(h) = 1 + N(h - 1) + N(h - 2), the Fibonacci recurrence. Fibonacci numbers grow like 1.618^h, so n &gt;= 1.618^h roughly, giving h &lt; 1.44 log2(n + 2).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Red-black trees: colours\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003cb>Invariant\u003c/b>: every node is red or black; the root is black; empty children count as black; a red node never has a red child; and every path from a node down to an empty child passes through the same number of black nodes (its black-height). The black nodes alone form a perfectly balanced skeleton, and reds can at most double a path's length (no two in a row), so \u003cb>h &lt;= 2 log2(n + 1)\u003c/b>. Repairs are recolourings plus at most two rotations per insert and three per delete, fewer than AVL, at the price of a slightly taller tree. That trade (cheaper updates, slightly slower lookups) is why general-purpose libraries chose it: Java's \u003ccode>TreeMap\u003c/code> is documented as red-black, C++'s \u003ccode>std::map\u003c/code> is usually one, and the Linux kernel uses rbtrees for timers and I/O schedulers. A red node can be read as glued to its black parent, making a node with 2 or 3 keys: red-black trees are B-trees with at most 3 keys per node in disguise.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Treaps: let randomness balance\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Give every key a \u003cb>random priority\u003c/b> at insertion. \u003cb>Invariant\u003c/b>: keys obey BST order, and priorities obey heap order (a parent's priority beats its children's). For distinct keys and priorities the tree is unique: it is exactly the plain BST you would get by inserting the keys in priority order, which is a random order. So the shape is a random BST whatever order the keys arrive in: expected depth about 1.39 log2 n, no worst case an adversary can force without seeing the random numbers. Insert as a leaf, then rotate it up while its priority beats its parent's. Treaps also support \u003cb>split\u003c/b> (by key) and \u003cb>merge\u003c/b> in expected O(log n), which makes range operations easy.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>B-trees: wide nodes, level leaves\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Each node holds many sorted keys and one more child than keys. With minimum degree t, every node except the root holds between t - 1 and 2t - 1 keys, and \u003cb>all leaves are at the same depth\u003c/b>. Insert into a leaf; if it overflows, \u003cb>split\u003c/b> it around its median and push the median up into the parent, which may split in turn. The tree grows only by splitting the root, which is why the leaves stay level. Height is at most about log base t of n: with hundreds of keys per node, three or four levels hold billions of keys. Each node is sized to a disk page or a few cache lines, so one node visit is one I/O. Deletions \u003cb>borrow\u003c/b> from or \u003cb>merge\u003c/b> with a sibling. The last card of this module shows how databases use the B+ variant.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Comparing them\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Design\u003c/th>\u003cth>Invariant\u003c/th>\u003cth>Height\u003c/th>\u003cth>Repair per update\u003c/th>\u003cth>Where you meet it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>AVL\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sibling heights differ by at most 1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>under 1.44 log2(n + 2)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>one single or double rotation per insert\u003c/td>\u003ctd>read-heavy in-memory indexes, teaching\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Red-black\u003c/td>\u003ctd>no red-red, equal black-heights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>at most 2 log2(n + 1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>recolour, at most 2 or 3 rotations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Java TreeMap, C++ std::map, Linux kernel rbtree\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Treap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BST by key, heap by random priority\u003c/td>\u003ctd>expected O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>rotate by priority\u003c/td>\u003ctd>randomised ordered sets, split and merge\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>B-tree, B+ tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>t - 1 to 2t - 1 keys per node, level leaves\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about log base t of n\u003c/td>\u003ctd>split, borrow, merge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>databases, file systems, Rust's BTreeMap\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Skip list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>random tower heights\u003c/td>\u003ctd>expected O(log n) levels\u003c/td>\u003ctd>none, coin flips\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Redis sorted sets up to 8.10, LevelDB memtables\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All of them make search, insert, delete, floor, ceiling and successor \u003cb>O(log n)\u003c/b> (worst case for AVL, red-black and B-trees; expected for treaps and skip lists). A rotation is O(1) and the fix-up walk O(log n). Augmentations (subtree sizes, sums, interval maxima) survive rotations because only two nodes' subtrees change, so their summaries are recomputed in O(1): that is how order-statistic and interval trees stay O(log n).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You almost never write one: use the language's ordered map (C++ \u003ccode>std::map\u003c/code>, Java \u003ccode>TreeMap\u003c/code>, Rust \u003ccode>BTreeMap\u003c/code>) or, in Python, \u003ccode>sortedcontainers\u003c/code> (next card). Know the ideas to predict costs and pick variants: AVL when lookups dominate, red-black for general use, treaps for split and merge, B-trees whenever a node visit is expensive (disk pages, cache misses).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Insert 1 to 7 in order into an AVL tree. A plain BST would become a chain of height 7:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 1, 2, 3. Node 1 now has an empty left subtree (height 0) and a right subtree of height 2: the rule is broken at 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>1\n \\\n  2\n   \\\n    \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The heavy side leans the same way all the way down (right, right), so one left rotation at 1 fixes it. In-order is still 1 2 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\n / \\\n1   3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 4: heights at 3 are 0 and 1, at 2 they are 1 and 2. Still within one everywhere, no rotation\u003c/p>\u003cpre>  2\n / \\\n1   3\n     \\\n      \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 5: node 3 has heights 0 and 2. Rotate left at 3: 4 moves up with children 3 and 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>  2\n / \\\n1   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\n   / \\\n  3   5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 6: now the root is unbalanced (left height 1, right height 3). Rotate left at 2: 4 becomes the root, and 3 moves across to be 2's right child\u003c/p>\u003cpre>    \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\n   / \\\n  2   5\n / \\   \\\n1   3   6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 7: node 5 has heights 0 and 2. Rotate left at 5. Four rotations in all, and the result is the perfect tree of height 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>    4\n   / \\\n  2   \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\n / \\ / \\\n1  3 5  7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\n\n\ndef avl_height(node):\n    \"\"\"Height of the subtree if every node's two subtree heights differ by at most 1, else -1.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    left, right = avl_height(node.left), avl_height(node.right)\n    if left &lt; 0 or right &lt; 0 or abs(left - right) &gt; 1:\n        return -1\n    return 1 + max(left, right)\n\n\ndef black_height(node):\n    \"\"\"Black-height of a subtree whose nodes have a boolean .red, or -1 if a colour rule is broken.\"\"\"\n    if node is None:\n        return 1                                   # empty children count as black\n    if node.red and any(c is not None and c.red for c in (node.left, node.right)):\n        return -1                                  # a red node with a red child\n    left, right = black_height(node.left), black_height(node.right)\n    if left &lt; 0 or left != right:\n        return -1                                  # unequal black counts below this node\n    return left + (0 if node.red else 1)\n\n\ndef is_red_black(root):\n    return (root is None or not root.red) and black_height(root) &gt; 0\n\n\ndef min_avl_nodes(h):\n    \"\"\"Fewest nodes in an AVL tree of height h: N(h) = 1 + N(h - 1) + N(h - 2).\"\"\"\n    if h == 0:\n        return 0\n    a, b = 0, 1                                    # N(0), N(1)\n    for _ in range(h - 1):\n        a, b = b, 1 + a + b\n    return b\n\n\ndef max_avl_height(n):\n    \"\"\"Tallest AVL tree that n nodes can form.\"\"\"\n    h = 0\n    while min_avl_nodes(h + 1) &lt;= n:\n        h += 1\n    return h\n\n\nprint([min_avl_nodes(h) for h in range(1, 11)])   # [1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20, 33, 54, 88, 143]\nprint(max_avl_height(10**6), math.ceil(math.log2(10**6 + 1)))   # 28 20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These checkers are what you would run after every operation in a property test of your own balanced tree (combined with the BST check from the validation card): they test the invariant, not the implementation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rotating without updating heights or colours\u003c/b>: the shape changes but the stored heights become stale, and the next fix-up makes the wrong decision. Recompute the two rotated nodes bottom-up.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Single rotation in the zig-zag case\u003c/b>: when the heavy child leans the other way (left child with a heavy right side), one rotation just moves the imbalance to the other side. It needs the double rotation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the parent link\u003c/b>: after rotating a subtree, its parent (or the root pointer) must point to the new subtree root. The \"return the new subtree root\" style from insert and delete makes this automatic.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Treating \"balanced\" as \"perfect\"\u003c/b>: AVL and red-black trees allow some lopsidedness; their height bounds are about 1.44 and 2 times the perfect height, not equal to it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Using a binary tree for on-disk data\u003c/b>: a lookup in a balanced binary tree of 10^9 keys visits about 30 nodes, each potentially a separate disk read; a B-tree visits 3 or 4.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Insert 3, then 1, then 2 into an empty AVL tree. Which node is unbalanced, which rotation fixes it, and what is the result?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>After inserting 2, node 3 has a left subtree of height 2 (1 with right child 2) and an empty right subtree. The heavy child 1 leans right while 3 leans left: the zig-zag case. Rotate left at 1 (making 2 the left child of 3, with 1 below it), then rotate right at 3. The result is 2 with children 1 and 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a red-black tree's height never exceed twice the minimum possible height?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every root-to-empty path has the same number b of black nodes, so the black nodes alone form a perfectly balanced skeleton and the tree has at least 2^b - 1 nodes, giving b &lt;= log2(n + 1). Reds cannot be adjacent, so at most every other node on a path is red and a path has at most 2b nodes. Hence h &lt;= 2 log2(n + 1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a treap's expected height not depend on the order in which the keys arrive?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The shape of a treap is determined by the keys and their priorities alone: it equals the plain BST obtained by inserting the keys in order of priority. Priorities are random and independent of the keys, so that order is a uniformly random permutation, and a BST built from a random permutation has expected O(log n) height whatever order the keys were originally given in.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A database stores 10^9 keys. Compare the number of node visits for one lookup in a red-black tree and in a B-tree whose nodes hold about 500 keys.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The red-black tree has height between 30 and at most 2 log2(10^9 + 1), about 60, so 30 to 60 node visits, each possibly a random disk read. The B-tree has height about log base 250 to 500 of 10^9, which is 4 levels at most, and the top levels are usually cached in memory, so one or two disk reads. That gap is why disk-based indexes are B-trees.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>By hand, AVL\u003c/b>: insert 1 to 7 in order. Expected: 4 single left rotations (after inserting 3, 5, 6 and 7), final level order \u003ccode>[4, 2, 6, 1, 3, 5, 7]\u003c/code>, height 3. Then insert 3, 1, 2 into an empty tree. Expected: one double rotation and the tree \u003ccode>[2, 1, 3]\u003c/code>. Check your drawings on VisuAlgo in AVL mode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Height bounds\u003c/b>: run the template. Expected: \u003ccode>[1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20, 33, 54, 88, 143]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>28 20\u003c/code>: a million-key AVL tree is at most 28 levels deep, against 20 for a perfect tree and at most 39 for a red-black tree (2 log2(n + 1) is about 39.9).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Colour rules\u003c/b>: give \u003ccode>TreeNode\u003c/code> a \u003ccode>red\u003c/code> attribute and check three small trees with \u003ccode>is_red_black\u003c/code>. Expected: True for a black root with two red children, False when one of those red children gets a red child of its own, and False when the root itself is red.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know the invariants and height bounds of AVL, red-black, treap and B-tree designs, how rotations and splits restore them without changing the order, and how to test the invariants.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:bst/which-ordered-map-should-you-use-in-python-and-w":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Python has no built-in balanced tree, and in CPython you rarely want one. For mostly static data, keep a \u003cb>sorted list\u003c/b> and use \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> for floor, ceiling and ranges in O(log n). For data that changes, use \u003ccode>sortedcontainers.SortedList\u003c/code>: a \u003cb>list of sorted sublists\u003c/b> of about 1,000 items with an index of their maxima, so every operation is two C-level binary searches plus a short \u003ccode>memmove\u003c/code>. It is B-tree-like with two levels, and it beats pointer-based trees in Python on speed and memory: measured here, about twice as fast as even an unbalanced, randomly built BST, with 8 bytes per key against 56.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A balanced tree promises O(log n) by doing a little interpreted work at each of about log2 n levels: load an attribute, compare, branch, follow a pointer to a node somewhere else in memory. In CPython each of those steps is a bytecode instruction costing tens of nanoseconds. \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> does the same comparisons inside a C loop over a contiguous array of pointers, and shifting a thousand pointers with \u003ccode>memmove\u003c/code> takes well under a microsecond on modern hardware. So the structure with the \"worse\" big-O (an O(m) insert into a sublist of length m) wins, because its constant factor is two orders of magnitude smaller. Grant Jenks's docs put it bluntly: tree-based designs have better big-O, \"but that ignores the realities of today's software and hardware\".\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The ordered-set interface\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Whatever the implementation, you want: add, remove, contains, minimum and maximum, \u003cb>floor\u003c/b> (largest key &lt;= x), \u003cb>ceiling\u003c/b> (smallest key &gt;= x), predecessor and successor (strict versions), \u003cb>rank\u003c/b> (how many keys are smaller) and \u003cb>select\u003c/b> (the k-th key), and \u003cb>range iteration\u003c/b> (all keys in [lo, hi] in order).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\u003cth>Python\u003c/th>\u003cth>C++\u003c/th>\u003cth>Java\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ordered set or map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>SortedList\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>SortedDict\u003c/code> (sortedcontainers)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>std::set\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>std::map\u003c/code> (usually red-black)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>TreeSet\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>TreeMap\u003c/code> (red-black)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ceiling, smallest &gt;= x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s[s.bisect_left(x)]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s.lower_bound(x)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ceiling(x)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ceilingKey(x)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>successor, smallest &gt; x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s[s.bisect_right(x)]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s.upper_bound(x)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>higher(x)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>floor, largest &lt;= x\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s[s.bisect_right(x) - 1]\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>prev(s.upper_bound(x))\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>floor(x)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>k-th smallest, rank\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s[k]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>s.bisect_left(x)\u003c/code> in O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>not built in (policy-based trees in GCC)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>not built in\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>range\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>s.irange(lo, hi)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>lower_bound\u003c/code> then iterate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>subSet(lo, true, hi, true)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>only the minimum, repeatedly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heapq\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>std::priority_queue\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PriorityQueue\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>(Check the index is in range before reading \u003ccode>s[...]\u003c/code>; the floor of a key below the minimum, for example, does not exist.)\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Option 1: a plain sorted list with bisect\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>bisect_left(a, x)\u003c/code> is the first index with \u003ccode>a[i] &gt;= x\u003c/code> (ceiling position); \u003ccode>bisect_right(a, x)\u003c/code> the first with \u003ccode>a[i] &gt; x\u003c/code>, so \u003ccode>bisect_right(a, x) - 1\u003c/code> is the floor. Queries are O(log n) in C. Inserting with \u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code> is O(log n) to find the spot plus O(n) to shift the tail: perfectly fine for a few thousand updates, or when keys arrive mostly in increasing order (appending at the end shifts nothing), and painful for 10^5 random inserts into a growing list (measured below: about 3 seconds).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Option 2: SortedList, a list of lists\u003c/h5>\u003cp>From the implementation notes: \u003ccode>_lists\u003c/code> holds sorted sublists, \u003ccode>_maxes\u003c/code> holds the maximum of each sublist, and \u003ccode>_index\u003c/code> is a lazily built tree of sublist lengths for positional queries. To add x: bisect \u003ccode>_maxes\u003c/code> to choose the sublist, \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> into it, and if the sublist grows past twice the load factor (default 1,000), split it in half. Removal shrinks sublists and merges a sublist that falls below half the load with a neighbour. Lookup is two bisects. Positional access \u003ccode>s[k]\u003c/code> walks the \u003ccode>_index\u003c/code> tree (children of position i at 2i + 1 and 2i + 2, like a heap) in O(log(n/m)).\u003c/p>\u003cp>The library's analysis gives an amortised O(n^(1/3)) insert when the load is about the cube root of n, technically worse than O(log n). In practice, as the docs argue, log2 of a billion is about 30 while the cube root is 1,000: the list of lists only needs to be about 33 times faster per step, and in CPython it is.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Why trees lose in Python\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The implementation page lists four advantages, and you can measure each:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fewer allocations\u003c/b>: most inserts and deletes allocate nothing; a tree allocates a node per key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Dense pointers\u003c/b>: a sublist is a C array of pointers; a tree node with \u003ccode>__slots__\u003c/code> is a 56-byte object (measured with \u003ccode>tracemalloc\u003c/code>: 56 bytes per key for a plain BST against 8 to 9 for SortedList, not counting the keys themselves).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One pointer per item\u003c/b>, against at least two child pointers per node.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fast iteration\u003c/b>: walking contiguous arrays is what CPUs do best.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Add the interpreter: every level of a Python tree costs several bytecode instructions, while bisect runs in C.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Measured on 10^5 random integer keys in CPython 3.12 (median of three runs on a shared server; your absolute numbers will differ, the ratios should not):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Structure\u003c/th>\u003cth>Build by 10^5 adds\u003c/th>\u003cth>10^5 membership lookups\u003c/th>\u003cth>Memory per key\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>plain BST, random order (no rebalancing at all)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.51 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.41 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>56 bytes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>SortedList\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.32 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.19 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8 to 9 bytes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>list + \u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3.0 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.22 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>8 bytes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>set (no order)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.02 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.03 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 42 bytes (hash table slots)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A real balanced tree in Python would be slower than the plain BST row, since it does the same walk plus rebalancing work. On sorted input the plain BST collapses (8,000 sorted keys already take about 4 seconds), \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> becomes fast (0.05 s, since every insert is an append), and SortedList stays fast (0.11 s).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Static data, many queries: sorted list + \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Changing data with order queries (floor, ceiling, k-th, ranges): \u003ccode>SortedList\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>SortedDict\u003c/code>. It is a third-party package, missing from some judges and interview environments, so also know the \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> fallback and how to describe a balanced tree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Only the minimum or maximum, repeatedly: \u003ccode>heapq\u003c/code> (O(log n) push and pop, much lighter).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Membership only: \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>dict\u003c/code>, by an order of magnitude.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sliding windows that need \"is any value within d of x?\": an ordered set of the window, or buckets of width d + 1 (each value can only collide with its own bucket and the two neighbours), which is O(n) overall.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>SortedList internals with the load factor shrunk to 2 for the picture (sublists split when they exceed 2 x 2 = 4 items; the real default is 1,000). These states are what the library really produces:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 50, 10, 40, 20. Everything fits in one sorted sublist, and _maxes records its maximum\u003c/p>\u003cpre>_lists  [[10, 20, 40, 50]]\n_maxes  [50]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 30: bisect _maxes to pick sublist 0, insort into it. It now has 5 items, more than 4, so it splits after the first load (2) items\u003c/p>\u003cpre>_lists  [[10, 20], \u003cmark>[30, 40, 50]\u003c/mark>]\n_maxes  [\u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>, 50]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 60: it is larger than every maximum, so it goes to the last sublist, whose maximum becomes 60\u003c/p>\u003cpre>_lists  [[10, 20], [30, 40, 50, \u003cmark>60\u003c/mark>]]\n_maxes  [20, \u003cmark>60\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 70: the last sublist reaches 5 items and splits again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>_lists  [[10, 20], [30, 40], \u003cmark>[50, 60, 70]\u003c/mark>]\n_maxes  [20, \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>, 70]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add 25 and 35: bisect_left(_maxes, 25) = 1, so both go into the middle sublist. No split, no allocation\u003c/p>\u003cpre>_lists  [[10, 20], [\u003cmark>25\u003c/mark>, 30, \u003cmark>35\u003c/mark>, 40], [50, 60, 70]]\n_maxes  [20, 40, 70]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Query 35: bisect _maxes (sublist 1), then bisect inside it (position 2). Its rank is 2 + 2 = 4, so s.index(35) is 4 and s[4] is 35\u003c/p>\u003cpre>_lists  [[10, 20], [25, 30, \u003cmark>35\u003c/mark>, 40], [50, 60, 70]]\n_maxes  [20, \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>, 70]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from bisect import bisect_left, bisect_right\n\nfrom sortedcontainers import SortedList\n\n\ndef floor(keys, x):\n    \"\"\"Largest key &lt;= x in a sorted list, or None.\"\"\"\n    i = bisect_right(keys, x)\n    return keys[i - 1] if i else None\n\n\ndef ceiling(keys, x):\n    \"\"\"Smallest key &gt;= x in a sorted list, or None.\"\"\"\n    i = bisect_left(keys, x)\n    return keys[i] if i &lt; len(keys) else None\n\n\ndef near_duplicate(nums, index_diff, value_diff):\n    \"\"\"Is there a pair i != j with |i - j| &lt;= index_diff and |nums[i] - nums[j]| &lt;= value_diff?\"\"\"\n    window = SortedList()                         # the last index_diff values\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        j = window.bisect_left(x - value_diff)    # smallest value &gt;= x - value_diff\n        if j &lt; len(window) and window[j] &lt;= x + value_diff:\n            return True\n        window.add(x)\n        if i &gt;= index_diff:\n            window.remove(nums[i - index_diff])\n    return False\n\n\nkeys = [10, 20, 30, 40]\nprint(floor(keys, 25), ceiling(keys, 25), floor(keys, 5), ceiling(keys, 40))   # 20 30 None 40\nscores = SortedList([50, 10, 40, 20, 30])\nscores.add(35)\nprint(list(scores.irange(20, 40)), scores.index(35), scores[-1])   # [20, 30, 35, 40] 3 50\nprint(near_duplicate([8, 1, 15, 4, 9], 2, 3), near_duplicate([8, 1, 15, 4, 9], 1, 3))   # True False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>pip install sortedcontainers\u003c/code> if it is missing; it is pure Python with no dependencies.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> in a hot loop\u003c/b>: each call shifts the tail of the list, so 10^5 to 10^6 random inserts become seconds to minutes. Switch to SortedList.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mutating keys inside a sorted structure\u003c/b>: if an object's comparison result changes after insertion, the order is silently broken. Remove, change, re-add.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Off-by-one in floor and ceiling\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code> decides whether an equal key counts. Write both helpers once, test them on keys that are present, absent, below the minimum and above the maximum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>std::lower_bound(s.begin(), s.end(), x)\u003c/code> on a \u003ccode>std::set\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: it does O(log n) comparisons but O(n) iterator steps, because set iterators are not random access. Use the member \u003ccode>s.lower_bound(x)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Assuming availability\u003c/b>: sortedcontainers is third-party. On a judge without it, a sorted list with bisect (small n) or a heap with lazy deletion (only min or max needed) are the usual fallbacks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">With \u003ccode>keys = [10, 20, 30, 40]\u003c/code>, what are \u003ccode>bisect_left(keys, 30)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bisect_right(keys, 30)\u003c/code>, the floor of 30 and the successor of 30?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>2 and 3. The floor is \u003ccode>keys[bisect_right - 1] = keys[2] = 30\u003c/code> (30 itself counts); the successor, the smallest key strictly greater, is \u003ccode>keys[bisect_right] = keys[3] = 40\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">SortedList's insert is O(m) for sublists of length m, worse than a tree's O(log n). Why is it still faster in CPython?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The O(m) part is a \u003ccode>memmove\u003c/code> of at most about 2,000 pointers in C, a fraction of a microsecond, and the searches are \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> calls in C. A Python tree does about log2 n levels of interpreted work (attribute loads, comparisons, branches) at tens of nanoseconds per bytecode, allocates a node object per key and chases pointers across memory. Constant factors of 30 to 100 dominate the asymptotic difference at any size that fits in memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need, for a stream of 10^5 prices, the number of earlier prices strictly below each new price. Which structure and which call?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A SortedList of earlier prices: for each price p, the answer is \u003ccode>s.bisect_left(p)\u003c/code> (the number of stored values below p), then \u003ccode>s.add(p)\u003c/code>. Both are about O(log n), so O(n log n) total. A plain list with \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> gives the same answers but O(n^2) shifting in the worst case; a Fenwick tree over compressed prices is the other standard answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Trace \u003ccode>near_duplicate([8, 1, 15, 4, 9], 1, 3)\u003c/code>. What is in the window when 4 is checked, and why is the answer False?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With index_diff 1 the window holds only the previous value. When 4 (index 3) is checked, the window is \u003ccode>[15]\u003c/code>; the smallest window value &gt;= 4 - 3 = 1 is 15, which is greater than 4 + 3 = 7, so no match. Every adjacent pair differs by more than 3 (7, 14, 11, 5), so the function returns False.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>220\u003c/b>: an ordered window with \u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code> on the lower edge. Expected: \u003ccode>near_duplicate([8, 1, 15, 4, 9], 2, 3)\u003c/code> is True (1 and 4 are two apart and differ by 3), the same with index_diff 1 is False, and \u003ccode>near_duplicate([5, 5], 1, 0)\u003c/code> is True; O(n log k) time for window size k. Then write the bucket version (bucket id \u003ccode>x // (value_diff + 1)\u003c/code>, check the same and the two neighbouring buckets) and stress-test both against the O(n k) double loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Floor and ceiling helpers\u003c/b>: expected \u003ccode>floor(keys, 25), ceiling(keys, 25), floor(keys, 5), ceiling(keys, 40)\u003c/code> print \u003ccode>20 30 None 40\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>keys = [10, 20, 30, 40]\u003c/code>; add \u003ccode>ceiling(keys, 41)\u003c/code>, which must return None.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to answer floor, ceiling, rank and range queries in Python with bisect and SortedList, what C++ and Java provide instead, and why a list of sorted lists beats a tree in CPython.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:bst/how-do-databases-and-redis-keep-huge-ordered-dat":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Storage rewards reading one big block over many small ones, so database indexes are \u003cb>B+ trees\u003c/b>: interior pages hold hundreds of separator keys that route the search, the rows (or row pointers) live in the \u003cb>leaves\u003c/b>, and leaves are \u003cb>linked to their siblings\u003c/b>, so a range query is one descent plus a sequential walk. With a fan-out of a few hundred, three or four levels cover a billion rows. In memory, the trade-offs differ: Redis sorted sets pair a \u003cb>hash table\u003c/b> (member to score) with an ordered structure, a skip list up to Redis 8.10 and a packed B+ tree for large sets in 8.12.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A library card catalogue has drawers labelled with ranges (\"Ka to Ke\"), and inside each drawer the cards are in order. To find one author you read the labels, open one drawer and flip to the card: two steps, not thirty. To list every author starting with K you find the first drawer and keep reading cards, moving to the next drawer when one runs out, never going back to the labels. That is a B+ tree: wide nodes so the path is short, and linked leaves so scans are sequential.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Why wide nodes\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Disks and SSDs read in pages, and even RAM is read in 64-byte cache lines, so fetching one key from a block costs nearly as much as fetching all of them. A balanced binary tree with a billion keys has about 30 levels, each potentially a separate random read. A B-tree whose nodes are pages holding f keys has about log base f of n levels. SQLite's default page size has been 4096 bytes since version 3.12.0; PostgreSQL stores tables and indexes in pages of usually 8 kB. With keys of a few dozen bytes, an interior page holds hundreds of them.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>B+ trees in SQLite and PostgreSQL\u003c/h5>\u003cp>SQLite stores every table and every index as a b-tree in its file. An \u003cb>interior page\u003c/b> contains K keys and K + 1 pointers to child pages, where a pointer is a 32-bit page number. \u003cb>Table b-trees\u003c/b> use the 64-bit rowid as key and store all row data in the leaves; \u003cb>index b-trees\u003c/b> store just the indexed columns plus the rowid. Interior pages are therefore pure routing information, which is what makes their fan-out large.\u003c/p>\u003cp>PostgreSQL's B-tree implementation follows Lehman and Yao's algorithm: each page has a \u003cb>right-link\u003c/b> to its right sibling and a \u003cb>high key\u003c/b>, an upper bound on the keys allowed on that page. A search that arrives at a page which has just been split by another backend sees a search key above the high key and simply follows the right-link: no read locks are held while descending. The same sibling links (plus left-links for backward scans) make range scans walk the leaf level directly.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Range scans\u003c/h5>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>WHERE k BETWEEN 100 AND 200\u003c/code>, the database descends once to the leaf containing the first key &gt;= 100, then reads leaves left to right until it passes 200. The cost is about log_f n page reads for the descent plus m / B leaf pages for m matching keys with B per leaf. Without an index on k, the only plan is to scan the whole table. SQLite shows the difference in its query plans: \u003ccode>SEARCH t USING INDEX idx_k (k&gt;? AND k&lt;?)\u003c/code> with the index, \u003ccode>SCAN t\u003c/code> without.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Because an index is sorted by its columns in order, a composite index on (last_name, first_name) serves \u003ccode>last_name = ?\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>last_name = ? AND first_name BETWEEN ...\u003c/code> with one descent and a scan, but not \u003ccode>first_name = ?\u003c/code> alone, whose matches are spread across the whole leaf level.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Redis sorted sets\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A sorted set must answer two different questions fast: \"what is this member's score?\" and \"which members are in this score or rank range?\". No single ordered tree does the first in O(1), so Redis keeps two indexes. Up to Redis 8.10, \u003ccode>t_zset.c\u003c/code> adds every element to a hash table mapping members to scores and to a skip list ordered by score, described in its header as \"almost a C translation\" of William Pugh's skip list, modified to allow repeated scores, to break ties by comparing members, and to keep a back pointer for reverse traversal. (Small sorted sets use a compact listpack encoding instead.) In the 8.12 release candidate of 29 October 2026, the header says large sorted sets \"use the packed B+ tree and compact member hash index implemented in zset_btree.c\", with leaves linked in both directions, chosen for memory efficiency; the redis.io page still describes the skip list. Documentation lags code, so check the source of the version you run.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Skip lists remain the memtable of LevelDB and RocksDB, and the storage module covers them, LSM trees and B-tree page management in depth.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Height of a B+ tree with n rows, B rows per leaf page and fan-out f: about 1 + ceil(log base f of (n / B)) levels. For n = 10^8, B = 100 and f = 300: 10^6 leaves, then 3,334, 12 and 1 interior pages, so \u003cb>4 levels\u003c/b>. For 10^9 rows it is still 4 (10^7 leaves, then 33,334, 112 and 1). The root and the second level are almost always cached, so a point lookup costs one or two real page reads. The equivalent balanced binary tree has about 27 to 30 levels.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Updates cost more than reads: every insert must update every index on the table, and a full leaf splits into two pages, pushing a separator key into the parent. Keys that arrive in increasing order (timestamps, sequences) keep landing on the rightmost leaf; random keys (such as random UUIDs) scatter inserts across all leaves, so more pages must stay in cache and more of them split.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Workload\u003c/th>\u003cth>Structure\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Point lookups only, in memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>hash table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Python dict, Redis strings\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ordered queries, in memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>balanced tree, skip list, list of lists\u003c/td>\u003ctd>std::map, Redis sorted sets, SortedList\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ordered queries on disk, read-heavy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>B+ tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SQLite and PostgreSQL indexes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Very write-heavy on disk\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LSM tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>LevelDB, RocksDB, Cassandra (storage module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lookups by one key and order by another\u003c/td>\u003ctd>two indexes over the same data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Redis sorted sets: hash plus ordered structure\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A range query for keys in [20, 45] on a small B+ tree whose leaves are linked left to right:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>                 [ 30 | 60 ]\n               /      |      \\\n     [10 20] --&gt; [30 40 50] --&gt; [60 70 80]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read the root page. 20 &lt; 30, so the first matching key can only be under the leftmost child\u003c/p>\u003cpre>page reads  1\nroot        [ \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark> | 60 ]\noutput      []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read leaf [10 20]. Binary search inside the page finds 20, the first key &gt;= 20: emit it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>page reads  2\nleaf        [10 \u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>]\noutput      [\u003cmark>20\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The leaf is exhausted: follow its sibling link instead of going back up the tree. Emit 30 and 40\u003c/p>\u003cpre>page reads  3\nleaf        [\u003cmark>30\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark> 50]\noutput      [20, \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">50 &gt; 45, so the scan stops. Three page reads: one descent of height 2, then one extra leaf. A billion-row table would need about 4 for the descent and one more per leaf of results\u003c/p>\u003cpre>page reads  3\nleaf        [30 40 \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>]\noutput      [20, 30, 40]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\nfrom bisect import bisect_left\n\n\ndef btree_levels(n_rows, rows_per_leaf, fanout):\n    \"\"\"Levels of a B+ tree: one leaf level plus interior levels until a single root.\"\"\"\n    pages = max(1, math.ceil(n_rows / rows_per_leaf))\n    levels = 1\n    while pages &gt; 1:\n        pages = math.ceil(pages / fanout)\n        levels += 1\n    return levels\n\n\ndef range_scan(keys, lo, hi):\n    \"\"\"Keys in [lo, hi] from a sorted list: one binary search, then a sequential scan.\"\"\"\n    i = bisect_left(keys, lo)\n    while i &lt; len(keys) and keys[i] &lt;= hi:\n        yield keys[i]\n        i += 1\n\n\nprint(btree_levels(10**8, 100, 300), btree_levels(10**9, 100, 300), btree_levels(10**9, 1, 2))   # 4 4 31\nprint(list(range_scan([10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80], 20, 45)))   # [20, 30, 40]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>range_scan\u003c/code> is the same \"descend once, then walk in order\" shape as an index range scan, with the leaf chain replaced by consecutive list positions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Indexes are not free\u003c/b>: each one costs space and slows every insert, update and delete of the indexed columns. Index the queries you actually run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expecting a scan to use an index it cannot use\u003c/b>: a condition on the second column of a composite index, or a function applied to the column, usually means a full scan. Read the plan (\u003ccode>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN\u003c/code> in SQLite, \u003ccode>EXPLAIN\u003c/code> in PostgreSQL).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Thinking in binary-tree heights for disk data\u003c/b>: what matters is page reads, and a B+ tree's height barely grows with n.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Random primary keys on write-heavy tables\u003c/b>: they spread inserts and page splits over the whole index; time-ordered keys keep them local.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trusting documentation over source\u003c/b>: Redis's own docs describe the skip list while the 8.12 code uses a B+ tree for large sets. When performance matters, check the version you deploy.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A table has 10^9 rows, 100 rows fit in a leaf page and interior pages have a fan-out of 300. How many levels does its B+ tree have, and how many disk reads does a point lookup typically need?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>10^7 leaves, then 33,334, 112 and 1 interior pages: 4 levels. With the root and the second level cached in memory, a lookup reads about 2 pages from storage (the third level and the leaf), often fewer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a B+ tree link its leaves, when an in-order traversal of a plain BST manages without links?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An in-order traversal climbs back up through ancestors (with a stack or parent pointers) to reach the next subtree, and on disk every one of those ancestor visits can be a page read. Sibling links let a range scan go straight from one leaf page to the next, so after the first descent it reads only leaf pages, in order, which is also friendly to read-ahead.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A table has an index on (last_name, first_name). Which of these can use it as a range scan: \u003ccode>last_name = 'Lee'\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>first_name = 'Ana'\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>last_name = 'Lee' AND first_name &gt;= 'M'\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The first and the third. The index is sorted by last name, then first name, so all 'Lee' entries are contiguous, and within them first names are sorted. Rows with first name 'Ana' are scattered across all last names, so the second query cannot be answered by one descent and a scan.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a Redis sorted set need two structures, and what does each answer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Commands need both \"member to score\" (\u003ccode>ZSCORE\u003c/code>, and finding the old score on \u003ccode>ZADD\u003c/code> updates) and \"order by score\" (\u003ccode>ZRANGE\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ZRANGEBYSCORE\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ZRANK\u003c/code>). A hash table answers the first in O(1) but knows no order; the ordered structure (skip list, or B+ tree in 8.12) answers ranges and ranks in O(log n + m) but would need a search by score, not by member. Keeping both gives each command its best path, at the cost of updating two structures on every write.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Index heights\u003c/b>: run the template. Expected: \u003ccode>4 4 31\u003c/code> (a binary tree of a billion single-key nodes needs 31 levels counted this way, against 4 for the B+ tree), then \u003ccode>[20, 30, 40]\u003c/code>. Try 8 KB pages with 40-byte keys (fan-out about 200) and 100 rows per leaf: expected 5 levels for 10^9 rows and 4 for 10^8.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Read a real plan\u003c/b>: with Python's built-in \u003ccode>sqlite3\u003c/code>, create a table \u003ccode>t(k INTEGER, v TEXT)\u003c/code> with 100,000 rows and an index \u003ccode>idx_k\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, then run \u003ccode>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT v FROM t WHERE k BETWEEN 100 AND 200\u003c/code>. Expected (SQLite 3.45; wording varies slightly by version): a row containing \u003ccode>SEARCH t USING INDEX idx_k (k&gt;? AND k&lt;?)\u003c/code>. The same query on the unindexed column \u003ccode>v\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>SCAN t\u003c/code>. Time both queries and note the gap.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why disk indexes are B+ trees with linked leaves, how a range scan reads them, how to estimate an index's height, and how Redis combines a hash table with an ordered structure.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:bst/balanced-or-not-a-plain-bst-against-sortedlist-b":"\u003cp>You will build the simplest possible ordered set, break it with sorted input, prove it correct with a stress test, and race it against the structures Python programmers actually use. The numbers you get are the evidence behind the ordered-maps card.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 or newer, and \u003ccode>pip install sortedcontainers\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If you built the \u003ccode>dsakit\u003c/code> benchmark harness earlier in the guide, use its timing function; otherwise the helper below is enough.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save the core of a plain BST, iterative throughout so that degenerate trees cannot crash it:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import bisect\nimport random\nimport statistics\nimport time\nimport tracemalloc\n\nfrom sortedcontainers import SortedList\n\n\nclass Node:\n    __slots__ = (\"val\", \"left\", \"right\")\n\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val, self.left, self.right = val, None, None\n\n\ndef insert(root, key):\n    new = Node(key)\n    if root is None:\n        return new\n    node = root\n    while True:\n        if key &lt; node.val:\n            if node.left is None:\n                node.left = new\n                return root\n            node = node.left\n        elif key &gt; node.val:\n            if node.right is None:\n                node.right = new\n                return root\n            node = node.right\n        else:\n            return root\n\n\ndef contains(root, key):\n    node = root\n    while node is not None:\n        if key == node.val:\n            return True\n        node = node.left if key &lt; node.val else node.right\n    return False\n\n\ndef floor_bst(root, key):\n    \"\"\"Largest key &lt;= key, or None.\"\"\"\n    best, node = None, root\n    while node is not None:\n        if node.val &lt;= key:\n            best, node = node.val, node.right\n        else:\n            node = node.left\n    return best\n\n\ndef height(root):\n    best, stack = 0, [(root, 1)] if root else []\n    while stack:\n        node, depth = stack.pop()\n        best = max(best, depth)\n        stack += [(c, depth + 1) for c in (node.left, node.right) if c]\n    return best\n\n\ndef timed(fn, repeats=3):\n    \"\"\"Median wall-clock time of fn() over a few runs.\"\"\"\n    times = []\n    for _ in range(repeats):\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        fn()\n        times.append(time.perf_counter() - start)\n    return statistics.median(times)\n\n\ndef stress(trials=1000, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"Random inserts and queries, checked against SortedList as the model.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    for _ in range(trials):\n        root, model = None, SortedList()\n        for _ in range(rng.randint(1, 30)):\n            x = rng.randint(0, 20)\n            root = insert(root, x)\n            if x not in model:\n                model.add(x)\n            q = rng.randint(-1, 21)\n            i = model.bisect_right(q)\n            assert floor_bst(root, q) == (model[i - 1] if i else None)\n            assert contains(root, q) == (q in model)\n    print(\"stress test passed\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>stress()\u003c/code>. Then break \u003ccode>floor_bst\u003c/code> on purpose (change \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code>) and confirm the stress test fails on a tiny case; restore it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Heights.\u003c/b> For n = 1,000, 10,000 and 100,000, insert \u003ccode>random.sample(range(10 * n), n)\u003c/code> and record the height, next to log2 n. For sorted keys, use n = 1,000 to 8,000 only.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Doubling.\u003c/b> Time sorted inserts for n = 1,000, 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000 and compute the ratio between consecutive times.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Head to head.\u003c/b> With n = 100,000 random keys and 100,000 random queries, time building and querying: the plain BST (insert one by one), \u003ccode>SortedList\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>add\u003c/code> one by one), a list with \u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code>, and a \u003ccode>set\u003c/code>. Use \u003ccode>timed\u003c/code> and keep the medians.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Sorted input.\u003c/b> Repeat the build for \u003ccode>SortedList\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> with the keys sorted (skip the plain BST: it would take many minutes).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"7\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Memory.\u003c/b> Wrap each build in \u003ccode>tracemalloc.start()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()\u003c/code>, building from a list of keys that already exists, and divide by n.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"8\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Explain.\u003c/b> Read the Implementation Details page linked from the ordered-maps card and write five sentences explaining your table, using the words allocation, pointer, bisect and load factor.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Numbers from a shared Linux server running CPython 3.12; your absolute times will differ, the shapes should not.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>The stress test passes; the planted bug fails at once, on a query equal to a stored key (the only case where \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> differ).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Random-order heights of roughly 22, 32 and 41 for n = 1,000, 10,000 and 100,000: about two to two and a half times log2 n (10, 13.3, 16.6). Sorted-order height equals n.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorted inserts roughly quadruple each time n doubles (measured: 0.27 s, 1.2 s, 4.1 s for 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000 keys): quadratic, as predicted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>At n = 100,000 random keys: plain BST about 0.5 s to build and 0.4 s for the lookups; SortedList about 0.3 s and 0.2 s; \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> about 3 s to build (every insert shifts half the list on average) but the fastest lookups among the ordered options, 0.2 s; \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> about 0.02 s and 0.03 s, with no order at all.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sorted input: \u003ccode>insort\u003c/code> about 0.05 s (every insert is an append), SortedList about 0.1 s.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Memory per key, not counting the key objects: about 56 bytes for the BST (one \u003ccode>__slots__\u003c/code> node) against 8 to 9 for SortedList, the \"one pointer per item\" the documentation promises.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The verdict: even a plain BST that never pays for rebalancing, on its best-case random input, loses to SortedList in CPython on speed and memory, and a balanced tree written in Python would be slower still. Hand-written trees are for learning and for interviews; in Python production code, reach for \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>sortedcontainers\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:700":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given the root of a binary search tree and a target key, return the node containing that key, together with the subtree rooted there. Return \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> if the key is absent. In this tree, every key in a node's left subtree is smaller than the node's key, and every key in its right subtree is larger.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a root 4 with children 2 and 7, searching for 2 returns the node 2; searching for 5 returns \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keys are distinct, and the input obeys the search-tree ordering. The tree need not be balanced, so its height \u003ccode>h\u003c/code> may be as large as its number of nodes. Returning a node means returning the existing subtree, not constructing a new tree.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find a key in a tree whose left side is always smaller and right side always larger.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At each node one comparison rules out an entire subtree. Which one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Search and insert by the ordering invariant\u003c/p>\u003cp>Walk from the root: go left if the target is smaller, right if it is larger, and stop on equality or at None. No stack or recursion is needed.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(h) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse every node until finding the key. A DFS takes O(n) time and O(h) stack space even when the ordering could discard most of the tree. The bottleneck is exploring branches that the ordering has already ruled out.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At a node larger than the target, the target cannot lie in its right subtree. At a smaller node, it cannot lie in its left subtree. Follow the only possible child until finding the key or reaching an empty pointer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Search 5 at root 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>5 &gt; 4: go right to 7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At 7, 5 &lt; 7: go left\u003c/p>\u003cpre>the left pointer is empty\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">No candidate subtree remains\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>None\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The remaining pointer always roots the only subtree that can still contain the target.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def search_bst(root, target):\n    node = root\n    while node is not None:\n        if target == node.val:\n            return node\n        node = node.left if target &lt; node.val else node.right\n    return None\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At most one node per level is examined: O(h) time and O(1) auxiliary space. This is O(log n) time for a balanced tree but O(n) for a chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the matching node, not only its value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty tree or absent key returns \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not assume the tree is balanced.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Insert an absent key at the empty pointer reached by the search.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the smallest key greater than or equal to the target, remembering candidates on the path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count how many comparisons a search takes in balanced and skewed trees.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:938":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Add the keys in a binary search tree that fall within an inclusive interval \u003ccode>[low, high]\u003c/code>. Keys outside the interval contribute nothing. For a tree with keys 3, 5 and 8, the interval \u003ccode>[4, 8]\u003c/code> has sum 13.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keys in the left subtree of a node are smaller than its key, and keys in the right subtree are larger. The endpoints are included. A tree can be skewed, so an iterative traversal avoids depending on Python's recursion limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sum of the keys of a search tree that fall inside [low, high].\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When a node's key is below low, what do you know about everything in its left subtree?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Search and insert by the ordering invariant\u003c/p>\u003cp>Recurse with pruning: below low, only the right subtree can contribute; above high, only the left; inside the range, add the key and recurse into both. You visit the matching keys plus the two boundary paths.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(h + k) time for k keys in range (O(n) in the worst case), O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Visit all \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> nodes and add a key whenever it lies between the bounds. This takes O(n) time and O(h) traversal space. It may inspect large subtrees whose keys are all too small or all too large.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If a node is below \u003ccode>low\u003c/code>, its entire left subtree is also below \u003ccode>low\u003c/code>. If it is above \u003ccode>high\u003c/code>, its entire right subtree is above \u003ccode>high\u003c/code>. Only visit a child when that side can contain a key in the interval.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At root 5 with interval [4, 8]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>add 5; both children could contain matches\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At left child 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>3 is below 4; skip its left subtree\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At right child 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>add 8; skip its right subtree\ntotal \u003cmark>13\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every skipped subtree is excluded by a strict search-tree ordering bound; every potentially matching child remains on the stack.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def range_sum_bst(root, low, high):\n    total = 0\n    stack = [root] if root is not None else []\n    while stack:\n        node = stack.pop()\n        if low &lt;= node.val &lt;= high:\n            total += node.val\n        if node.left is not None and node.val &gt; low:\n            stack.append(node.left)\n        if node.right is not None and node.val &lt; high:\n            stack.append(node.right)\n    return total\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> keys match, the traversal visits those keys and boundary search paths: O(h + k) time, or O(n) in the worst case. The DFS stack uses O(h) auxiliary space. Skewed trees can therefore still require linear time and space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use inclusive comparisons for the sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A subtree may be skipped only because the input really is a search tree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty tree or interval containing no keys has sum zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the matching keys in sorted order using a pruned in-order walk.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Answer many intervals using prefix sums over an in-order array.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Maintain subtree sums in a dynamically updated tree.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:108":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a height-balanced binary search tree from a sorted array of distinct integers. A height-balanced tree has child subtree heights differing by at most one at every node. The exact valid shape is not unique.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[-3, 0, 4]\u003c/code>, choosing 0 as the root, -3 as its left child and 4 as its right child produces a valid balanced tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The values are already in strictly increasing order. Choosing the first value repeatedly would form a chain, so the root must divide the remaining input into comparably sized portions. Avoid copying array slices at each recursive call.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Turn a sorted array into a search tree whose height is as small as possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which element should be the root so that the two sides end up the same size?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Balanced build from sorted keys\u003c/p>\u003cp>Make the middle element the root and build the left and right halves recursively over index ranges. At every node the two sides differ in size by at most one, so the height is ceil(log2(n + 1)).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(log n) recursion depth\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Insert the values into an ordinary binary search tree in their existing sorted order. This produces a skewed tree, not the required balanced tree, and takes O(n²) comparisons. Trying every possible tree shape and checking its balance would find a valid shape but has combinatorial cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the middle element as the root and apply the same construction to each half. All elements in the left half are smaller and all elements in the right half are larger. Halving at each level keeps the subtree heights balanced.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Array [-3, 0, 4]: select the middle key\u003c/p>\u003cpre>root \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Build each remaining half\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left [-3] -&gt; -3    right [4] -&gt; 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Both child subtrees have height 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return the balanced tree\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each recursive call owns a half-open index range. Its two subranges are disjoint, differ in length by at most one, and together contain all remaining values.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = left\n        self.right = right\n\n\ndef sorted_array_to_bst(values):\n    def build(start, end):\n        if start == end:\n            return None\n        middle = (start + end) // 2\n        return TreeNode(values[middle], build(start, middle), build(middle + 1, end))\n\n    return build(0, len(values))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One node is created per value, so time and output space are O(n). Balanced recursion has O(log n) auxiliary stack space; no array slices are created.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use the same half-open range convention for both children.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> for an empty range.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Distinguish the required output nodes from auxiliary stack space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Construct a balanced search tree from an in-order linked list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the lower middle rather than the upper middle when the range length is even.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Verify that the in-order traversal equals the input and every subtree is balanced.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:235":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given two nodes in a binary search tree, return their lowest common ancestor: the deepest node that has both targets in its subtree. A node is considered an ancestor of itself. Both target nodes are present and keys are distinct.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If root 6 has children 2 and 8, the lowest common ancestor of 2 and 8 is 6. If one target is 2 and the other is a descendant of 2, the answer is 2 itself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree is ordered, the target keys are distinct, and both nodes occur in the tree. The height may be linear in the number of nodes, because a binary search tree is not necessarily balanced. Comparing the keys gives enough information to choose a direction without traversing both sides.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Lowest common ancestor of two nodes, in a tree where the keys are ordered.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Compare both keys with the current node. When can you be sure that the answer is not further down?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Search and insert by the ordering invariant\u003c/p>\u003cp>From the root, go left while both keys are smaller and right while both are larger. The first node where they part (or that equals one of them) is the lowest common ancestor; no subtree ever needs to be searched.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(h) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find and store the root-to-target path for each node, then compare the paths until they diverge. In a search tree each path takes O(h) time and O(h) space. A more general binary-tree DFS can scan O(n) nodes. The extra path storage is unnecessary when ordering already identifies the split point.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At the current node, if both target keys are smaller, their lowest common ancestor must be in the left subtree. If both are larger, it must be in the right subtree. Otherwise the targets split across the node or one equals it; this node is their lowest common ancestor.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Search for the ancestor of 2 and 8, starting at 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>2 &lt; 6 &lt; 8\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The targets lie on opposite sides\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>When both targets fall on the same side, the discarded side cannot contain either one, so it cannot contain their lowest common ancestor. At the first split, the current node contains both targets, while neither of its children contains both. This proves it is the lowest such node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def bst_lowest_common_ancestor(root, p, q):\n    low, high = sorted((p.val, q.val))\n    node = root\n    while node is not None:\n        if high &lt; node.val:\n            node = node.left\n        elif low &gt; node.val:\n            node = node.right\n        else:\n            return node\n    raise ValueError(\"the targets must be present in the search tree\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Only one root-to-leaf path is followed, so time is O(h), with O(1) auxiliary space. That is O(log n) in a balanced tree and O(n) in a chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Equality is a valid split: a target can be its own ancestor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use both keys when choosing a direction, not just one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>This reasoning requires search-tree ordering and the promised target membership.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Find the lowest common ancestor in an arbitrary binary tree without ordering.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the distance between two nodes after locating their ancestor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For many static tree queries, preprocess binary-lifting ancestor tables.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:98":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Decide whether a binary tree is a valid binary search tree. Every node's entire left subtree must contain strictly smaller keys, and its entire right subtree strictly larger keys. Equal keys are not permitted.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The tree with root 5, right child 7 and that child's left child 4 is invalid: 4 is locally smaller than 7, but it is in the right subtree of 5 and therefore must also be greater than 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input is an arbitrary binary tree, not necessarily a search tree. Checking only a node and its immediate children cannot detect violations against more distant ancestors. Values may be negative or outside a chosen fixed sentinel range. A skewed input can have height \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decide whether a binary tree obeys the search-tree ordering everywhere, not just between parents and children.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A node deep in the right subtree of the root must still be larger than the root. What range must each node's key lie in?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Validation with bounds passed down\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pass an open interval (low, high) down: the left child gets (low, val) and the right child (val, high), and a key outside its interval fails. Equivalently, the in-order sequence must be strictly increasing.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At every node, traverse its complete left subtree and confirm every key is smaller, then traverse its complete right subtree and confirm every key is larger. Repeat for all nodes. A chain causes O(n²) total visits because the same descendants are checked for many ancestors. The repeated subtree scans are the bottleneck.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Carry the interval allowed by all ancestors down the tree. A left child inherits the lower bound and gains its parent's key as an exclusive upper bound; a right child gains the parent's key as an exclusive lower bound. If a node fails either bound, the tree is invalid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An alternative is to check that an in-order traversal is strictly increasing, but the explicit bounds make the ancestor constraint visible.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Root 5 has no bounds\u003c/p>\u003cpre>valid; its right child must be &gt; 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 7 is in interval (5, infinity)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>valid; its left child must be in (5, 7)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 4 is not greater than 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>false\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>By induction, the interval on each stack entry contains exactly the restrictions imposed by its ancestors. If all nodes satisfy them, every node is correctly ordered with respect to every ancestor, which is the search-tree property.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def is_valid_bst(root):\n    stack = [(root, None, None)] if root is not None else []\n    while stack:\n        node, low, high = stack.pop()\n        if low is not None and node.val &lt;= low:\n            return False\n        if high is not None and node.val &gt;= high:\n            return False\n        if node.right is not None:\n            stack.append((node.right, node.val, high))\n        if node.left is not None:\n            stack.append((node.left, low, node.val))\n    return True\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is pushed and popped at most once, so time is O(n). Depth-first traversal holds at most O(h) pending nodes for tree height \u003ccode>h\u003c/code>. The explicit stack avoids recursion-depth failures, but a chain can still use O(n) space in the worst-case height bound.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Bounds are strict: duplicate keys make the tree invalid.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Checking only parent-child relationships misses ancestor violations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>None\u003c/code> denotes an absent bound, so valid integers are not confused with arbitrary finite sentinels.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Validate by checking that in-order keys are strictly increasing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If duplicates are allowed on exactly one side, change the relevant bound to inclusive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the first node that violates its allowed interval.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:450":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Remove a given key from a binary search tree and return the resulting root. If the key is not present, leave the tree unchanged. The remaining nodes must still satisfy the strict search-tree ordering.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a root 5 with left child 3 and right subtree rooted at 7 with left child 6, deleting 5 can replace its value with 6, the smallest value in its right subtree, then remove the original node 6.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keys are distinct, and the input is a valid binary search tree. The root itself may need to be removed, so a deletion can change the root pointer. A node has zero, one or two children; the two-child case cannot simply discard either subtree.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove one key from a search tree so that it is still a valid search tree.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Removing a leaf or a node with one child is easy. With two children, which key could take the node's place without breaking the order?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Deletion with successor replacement\u003c/p>\u003cp>Recurse and return the new subtree root. At the key: no left child, return the right; no right child, return the left; otherwise copy the minimum of the right subtree (the in-order successor) into the node and delete that key from the right subtree.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(h) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse the tree, collect all keys except the one to remove, and construct a new search tree from those keys. This takes at least O(n) time and O(n) storage for the list and reconstructed nodes. It also replaces the original tree rather than editing only the affected path. The full traversal and rebuilding are the bottleneck when the tree is large and balanced.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the target using the search-tree ordering. If it has at most one child, replace its parent's pointer with that child. If it has two children, copy the in-order successor's value into the target: the successor is the smallest node in the right subtree, so it is greater than every key on the left and no larger than any remaining key on the right. Then remove the successor from its original location. The successor has no left child, reducing the problem to the one-child case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Delete root 5 with children 3 and 7; 7 has left child 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>successor is \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Copy 6 into the target and remove the original leaf 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>root 6, left 3, right 7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">All remaining keys are ordered\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return the updated root\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The successor is the smallest key greater than the deleted key, so substituting it preserves both subtree bounds. Removing its original node avoids leaving a duplicate. The other pointers are unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def delete_bst(root, key):\n    parent = None\n    node = root\n    while node is not None and node.val != key:\n        parent = node\n        node = node.left if key &lt; node.val else node.right\n    if node is None:\n        return root\n\n    if node.left is not None and node.right is not None:\n        successor_parent = node\n        successor = node.right\n        while successor.left is not None:\n            successor_parent = successor\n            successor = successor.left\n        node.val = successor.val\n        parent, node = successor_parent, successor\n\n    child = node.left if node.left is not None else node.right\n    if parent is None:\n        return child\n    if parent.left is node:\n        parent.left = child\n    else:\n        parent.right = child\n    return root\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The search path and the successor path together have length O(h), where \u003ccode>h\u003c/code> is tree height. The algorithm uses O(1) auxiliary space and modifies the tree in place. In a skewed tree, O(h) can be O(n).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the replacement child when deleting a root with at most one child.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Delete the successor from its old location after copying its value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The successor may be the target's immediate right child or may itself have a right child.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Searching for a missing key must leave the tree unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use the in-order predecessor from the left subtree instead of the successor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return whether a deletion occurred as well as the new root.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Maintain subtree sizes to support order-statistic queries after deletion.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:230":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>-th smallest key in a binary search tree, using one-based \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. For a tree whose sorted keys are \u003ccode>[1, 3, 5, 8]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>k = 3\u003c/code> returns 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree has distinct keys and \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is within the number of nodes. An in-order traversal of a valid search tree visits keys in strictly increasing order. The tree may be skewed, so an explicit stack avoids relying on recursion depth.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The k-th smallest key of a search tree.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which traversal visits a search tree's keys in increasing order, and do you have to finish it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sorted order from in-order traversal\u003c/p>\u003cp>Iterative in-order with a stack: push the left spine, pop and count, and return at the k-th pop. If such queries repeat while the tree changes, store subtree sizes in the nodes and answer each in O(h).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(h + k) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse all \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> nodes, collect their values, sort them, and read position \u003ccode>k - 1\u003c/code>. This uses O(n log n) time and O(n) space. Even a full in-order traversal and list uses O(n) time and space; neither needs to keep visiting nodes after the requested rank has been reached.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Perform in-order traversal lazily. Push the current node's left spine, pop the next smallest node, and then move to its right subtree. Decrease \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> on every pop. The node popped when \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> reaches zero is the answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sorted order of the tree is 1, 3, 5, 8; request k = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pop 1: remaining rank 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remaining rank 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remaining rank 0; return \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The stack holds ancestors whose left subtrees have been handled but which have not yet been emitted. Popping therefore yields the next smallest key, and exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> pops reach the desired rank.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, after visiting a node with a right child, do not immediately return that child's key: its own left subtree may contain smaller keys. Following that child's left spine before the next pop restores the correct order. The algorithm stops as soon as the answer appears, so a small rank need not require traversing the rest of the tree. Already visited nodes need not remain on the stack.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def kth_smallest(root, k):\n    if k &lt; 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"k must be positive\")\n    stack = []\n    node = root\n    while node is not None or stack:\n        while node is not None:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        k -= 1\n        if k == 0:\n            return node.val\n        node = node.right\n    raise ValueError(\"k exceeds the number of nodes\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The traversal visits the first \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> keys and at most one additional left spine, for O(h + k) time and O(h) stack space. The worst case is O(n) time and O(n) space on a skewed tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is one-based; decrement on a pop, not on a push.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Visit a popped node before its right subtree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For repeated queries on an unchanged tree, consider preprocessing the sorted keys rather than repeating the walk.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Yield all keys lazily with the same stack.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>-th largest by reversing left and right.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Maintain subtree sizes to answer rank queries in O(h) as the tree changes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:173":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Create an iterator over a binary search tree that returns its keys in ascending order. \u003ccode>has_next()\u003c/code> reports whether another key remains; \u003ccode>next()\u003c/code> returns that key and advances the iterator.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a tree with keys 2, 5 and 8, three calls to \u003ccode>next()\u003c/code> return 2, 5 and 8. Afterwards \u003ccode>has_next()\u003c/code> is false.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree remains unchanged while the iterator is in use. The goal is O(h) iterator space, where \u003ccode>h\u003c/code> is tree height, rather than storing every key. An individual operation may walk a long left spine, so distinguish worst-case per-call time from amortised time over the full iteration.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Hand out a search tree's keys one at a time in increasing order, with memory proportional to the height.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pause an in-order traversal between calls. What state does it need to keep?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sorted order from in-order traversal\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the in-order stack in the object. The constructor pushes the left spine of the root; next pops a node, pushes the left spine of its right child and returns the key. Each node is pushed and popped once, so next is O(1) amortised.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) amortised per call, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse the whole tree in-order in the constructor, save all keys in an array, and return them by index. The constructor takes O(n) time and stores O(n) keys even if the caller requests only one. The eager materialisation is the bottleneck in memory and startup work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Pause an iterative in-order traversal between calls. Store a stack of nodes whose left subtrees have been explored but which have not been returned. The constructor pushes the root's left spine. Each \u003ccode>next()\u003c/code> pops the next node and pushes the left spine of that node's right subtree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Tree root 5, left 2, right 8: push the left spine\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack [5, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 2; it has no right subtree\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>; stack [5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 5, then push the left spine of its right child 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>; stack [8]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>; stack empty\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>At every step, the top of the stack is the smallest not-yet-returned key. Pushing a right subtree's left spine restores that invariant after its predecessor is returned.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Calling \u003ccode>has_next()\u003c/code> only inspects the stack; it does not consume a key. This means repeated checks have no effect on the next value. The iterator assumes the underlying tree is not modified between calls, because its stored traversal state would otherwise become stale.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class BSTIterator:\n    def __init__(self, root):\n        self.stack = []\n        self._push_left(root)\n\n    def _push_left(self, node):\n        while node is not None:\n            self.stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n\n    def has_next(self):\n        return bool(self.stack)\n\n    def next(self):\n        if not self.stack:\n            raise StopIteration(\"the tree iterator is exhausted\")\n        node = self.stack.pop()\n        self._push_left(node.right)\n        return node.val\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The stack stores O(h) nodes. Each node is pushed once and popped once, so a complete iteration takes O(n) time: \u003ccode>next()\u003c/code> is O(1) amortised, though a particular call can take O(h) while pushing a left spine. The constructor can also take O(h).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Popping without pushing the right child's left spine skips keys.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty tree has no next value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not describe every \u003ccode>next()\u003c/code> as worst-case O(1); the guarantee is amortised.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Iterate in descending order by swapping left and right.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Make the class conform to Python's \u003ccode>__iter__\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>__next__\u003c/code> protocol.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Explore what extra coordination would be needed if the tree could change during iteration.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:99":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Exactly two values in a binary search tree have been swapped. Restore the search-tree ordering by swapping those values back, without changing the tree's shape.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For an in-order sequence that should be \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\u003c/code> but is now \u003ccode>[1, 4, 3, 2, 5]\u003c/code>, swap 4 and 2. The same method also handles adjacent swapped values, such as \u003ccode>[1, 3, 2, 4]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original keys are distinct and the tree shape was not changed. Its in-order traversal should therefore be strictly increasing once corrected. A tree can be skewed, so recursion may use O(n) stack frames. The task requires an in-place repair rather than constructing a replacement tree.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Exactly two keys of a search tree were swapped by mistake; fix it without changing the shape.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Compare the in-order sequence of the broken tree with a sorted one. What do the two misplaced keys leave behind?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sorted order from in-order traversal\u003c/p>\u003cp>In-order is sorted except for one or two descents (a key followed by a smaller one). The first culprit is the larger key of the first descent and the second is the smaller key of the last descent; swap their values. Morris traversal brings the extra space to O(1).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Collect all nodes in in-order, sort their values, and write the sorted values back into the nodes. This uses O(n log n) time and O(n) extra space. The sorting and stored list are unnecessary because only two values are wrong.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>During in-order traversal, look for a descent: a previous value greater than the current value. The larger value at the first descent is the first misplaced node. The smaller value at the last descent is the other misplaced node. Adjacent swapped values give one descent; non-adjacent swapped values give two. Swap those two values after traversal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">In-order values: 1, 4, 3, 2, 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first descent 4 &gt; 3: record first = 4, second = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second descent 3 &gt; 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>keep first = 4; update second = \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Swap the recorded values\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in-order becomes \u003cmark>1, 2, 3, 4, 5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In the original strictly increasing sequence, a too-large value moved left is followed by a smaller value, creating the first descent. A too-small value moved right is preceded by a larger value, creating the last. If they were adjacent, the same descent identifies both.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def recover_bst(root):\n    stack = []\n    node = root\n    previous = first = second = None\n    while node is not None or stack:\n        while node is not None:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        if previous is not None and previous.val &gt; node.val:\n            if first is None:\n                first = previous\n            second = node\n        previous = node\n        node = node.right\n    if first is None or second is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"expected exactly two swapped values\")\n    first.val, second.val = second.val, first.val\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The traversal visits all \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> nodes once, for O(n) time. The explicit stack uses O(h) auxiliary space for height \u003ccode>h\u003c/code>. A threaded Morris traversal can reduce auxiliary space, but temporarily changes pointers and needs extra care to restore them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep the first node from the first descent; update the second at every descent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not assume there are always two descents: adjacent swapped values produce only one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Swapping node links would change the shape; swap the two stored values instead.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Identify the two out-of-order values without modifying the tree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use Morris traversal to reduce auxiliary space while restoring every temporary thread.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check a repaired tree with the bounds-based validator.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:669":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Remove every key outside the inclusive interval \u003ccode>[low, high]\u003c/code> from a binary search tree and return the new root. Keep the remaining keys in a valid search tree.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For root 3 with left child 1 and right child 5, trimming to \u003ccode>[2, 4]\u003c/code> leaves only node 3. If the original root is outside the interval, a descendant can become the new root.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input is a valid search tree with distinct keys. Both interval endpoints are included. Whole subtrees can be discarded using the ordering: if a node is below \u003ccode>low\u003c/code>, its left subtree is also below \u003ccode>low\u003c/code>; the symmetric rule applies above \u003ccode>high\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove every key outside [low, high] from a search tree and return the new root.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If a node's key is below low, what happens to its left subtree, and what should take the node's place?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Deletion with successor replacement\u003c/p>\u003cp>Recurse and return the new subtree root: below low, return the trimmed right subtree; above high, return the trimmed left subtree; inside, trim both children and keep the node. The ordering removes whole subtrees at once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse every node, collect keys in the range, and build a new search tree from the retained sorted keys. This takes O(n) time plus O(n) storage for the traversal and new nodes. The full copy is unnecessary: the existing pointers can be adjusted along the interval boundaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Move the root down until it is in range: below \u003ccode>low\u003c/code> move right, and above \u003ccode>high\u003c/code> move left. Then trim the left boundary. If a node's left child is below \u003ccode>low\u003c/code>, replace that child by its right child, since the low-valued child's left subtree is entirely too small. Walk down the retained left side and repeat. Do the mirror operation on the right boundary for values above \u003ccode>high\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>All interior subtrees are already bounded by their ancestors and need no further processing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Root 3 lies in [2, 4]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>keep 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Its left child 1 is below 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>replace that child with its right subtree, here empty\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Its right child 5 is above 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>replace that child with its left subtree, here empty\nreturn \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Discarded branches cannot contain valid values by the search-tree ordering. The replacement child is the only side that might still contain valid keys, and linking it preserves the ancestor's ordering constraints.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def trim_bst(root, low, high):\n    while root is not None and (root.val &lt; low or root.val &gt; high):\n        root = root.right if root.val &lt; low else root.left\n    node = root\n    while node is not None:\n        while node.left is not None and node.left.val &lt; low:\n            node.left = node.left.right\n        node = node.left\n    node = root\n    while node is not None:\n        while node.right is not None and node.right.val &gt; high:\n            node.right = node.right.left\n        node = node.right\n    return root\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every followed or discarded node is visited at most a constant number of times, so time is O(n) in the worst case. The iterative pointer updates require O(1) auxiliary space. Entire out-of-range subtrees can be skipped without visiting their interiors.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Root adjustment must happen before adjusting its descendants.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep keys equal to either endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An interval containing no keys returns \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When removing a too-small left child, keep its right subtree, not its left.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return a sorted list of matching keys without changing the tree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Trim recursively and compare the stack-space trade-off on a skewed tree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count the retained keys while trimming.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1373":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given an arbitrary binary tree of integer values, find the largest sum among its subtrees that are valid binary search trees. A subtree contains a node and all its descendants. The empty subtree is allowed and has sum zero, so the answer is never negative.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If a root 5 has left child 3 and right child 8, the whole tree is a search tree with sum 16. If all node values are negative, choosing the empty subtree gives zero rather than a negative answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input is not guaranteed to be a search tree. A valid candidate needs strict ordering throughout both child subtrees, not merely between a node and its children. The tree may be deep, so a recursive implementation can exceed Python's recursion limit. Negative values mean a valid subtree's sum is not necessarily better than the empty choice.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The largest key sum of any subtree that is itself a valid search tree, inside a tree that need not be one.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A parent can tell whether it roots a valid search tree only if each child reports a few facts about itself. Which facts?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Validation with bounds passed down\u003c/p>\u003cp>Post-order returns (is_bst, min, max, sum) for each subtree, with the empty subtree as (True, +inf, -inf, 0). A node is valid when both children are and left max &lt; val &lt; right min; record its sum in a running maximum that starts at 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(h) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each node, independently validate its entire subtree using bounds and sum its keys if valid. On a chain, subtree sizes are \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>n-1\u003c/code>, and so on, giving O(n²) time. Rechecking descendants for each possible root is the bottleneck.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process nodes bottom-up. For every subtree record whether it is a search tree, its minimum key, its maximum key, and its sum. A node forms a valid search tree precisely when both child summaries are valid and \u003ccode>left_max &lt; node.val &lt; right_min\u003c/code>. In that case compute the new minimum, maximum and sum and update the best answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An empty child is valid with minimum positive infinity, maximum negative infinity and sum zero. These sentinels make the strict comparisons work without special cases. An invalid child makes its parent invalid regardless of its bounds. A separate best value starts at zero to represent the allowed empty subtree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Leaf 3 returns valid, min 3, max 3, sum 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Leaf 8 returns valid, min 8, max 8, sum 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best 8\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Root 5 sees left max 3 &lt; 5 &lt; right min 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>subtree sum \u003cmark>16\u003c/mark>; best \u003cmark>16\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The summary is sufficient: the child validity flags establish ordering inside each subtree, while the two extreme keys establish all cross-boundary comparisons with the parent. Conversely, if either child is invalid or an extreme violates the bound, the parent's full subtree cannot be valid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The explicit post-order stack first schedules a node for a later visit, then processes its children. On that later visit both summaries exist. This avoids dependence on the Python recursion limit; the summary table retains one record per node.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a leaf with value \u003ccode>-4\u003c/code>, the two empty summaries allow the leaf to be classified as valid with sum \u003ccode>-4\u003c/code>, but the global best stays zero. If a parent and child both have value 5, the strict comparison rejects the parent even though each leaf is valid by itself. Invalid summaries carry placeholder bounds that are never used unless their validity flag is true.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def maximum_sum_bst(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return 0\n    empty = (True, float(\"inf\"), float(\"-inf\"), 0)\n    summaries = {}\n    best = 0\n    stack = [(root, False)]\n    while stack:\n        node, visited = stack.pop()\n        if not visited:\n            stack.append((node, True))\n            if node.right is not None:\n                stack.append((node.right, False))\n            if node.left is not None:\n                stack.append((node.left, False))\n            continue\n        left = summaries.get(node.left, empty)\n        right = summaries.get(node.right, empty)\n        if left[0] and right[0] and left[2] &lt; node.val &lt; right[1]:\n            total = left[3] + node.val + right[3]\n            summaries[node] = (True, min(left[1], node.val), max(right[2], node.val), total)\n            best = max(best, total)\n        else:\n            summaries[node] = (False, 0, 0, 0)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is pushed and processed a constant number of times, so time is O(n) with expected O(1) dictionary operations. The summary table uses O(n) space, and the explicit traversal stack uses O(h) for tree height \u003ccode>h\u003c/code>. This is more space than a recursive implementation that returns child summaries without retaining a table, but it works on deep trees without changing the recursion limit.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Test both child validity flags before using their minimum and maximum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ordering is strict, so duplicates cannot belong to the same valid search-tree subtree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Start the best sum at zero: all-negative trees still have answer zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not confuse an arbitrary connected set of nodes with a subtree containing all descendants.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the root of the best subtree as well as its sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count valid search-tree subtrees instead of maximising their sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute the size of the largest valid search-tree subtree.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:220":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given an array and two non-negative limits, decide whether there are two distinct indices whose distance is at most \u003ccode>index_diff\u003c/code> and whose values differ by at most \u003ccode>value_diff\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[1, 5, 3]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>index_diff = 2\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>value_diff = 2\u003c/code>, the pair 1 and 3 qualifies. With the same values but \u003ccode>index_diff = 1\u003c/code>, neither adjacent pair qualifies.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The limits are inclusive, and the two indices must be different. Values can be negative, so a bucket calculation must handle negative numbers consistently. If \u003ccode>index_diff\u003c/code> is zero, no distinct pair can qualify. For each new value, only the previous \u003ccode>index_diff\u003c/code> values need to be considered.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Is there a pair of values within value_diff of each other whose indices are at most index_diff apart?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Slide a window over the last index_diff values. For a new value x, which single value of the window do you need to look at?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Ordered sets: floor, ceiling and ranges\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the window in an ordered set. For each x, find the smallest window value &gt;= x - value_diff (a ceiling query) and check that it is &lt;= x + value_diff; then add x and drop the value that left the window. Buckets of width value_diff + 1 make it O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log k) time with an ordered set (O(n) with buckets), O(k) space for window size k\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each index, compare its value with every earlier value within the allowed index distance. This costs O(nk) time for \u003ccode>k = min(index_diff, n)\u003c/code> and O(1) extra space. When the allowed distance is large, repeated comparisons with the same window become quadratic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use buckets of integer width \u003ccode>value_diff + 1\u003c/code>. Any two integers in the same bucket differ by at most \u003ccode>value_diff\u003c/code>, so finding an existing value in the new value's bucket immediately proves a match. A match can also straddle a bucket boundary, but only in the two neighbouring buckets; check the actual difference there.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep buckets only for the previous \u003ccode>index_diff\u003c/code> positions. Check the current value before inserting it, so it cannot match itself. After inserting, evict the value that will be too old for the next iteration. If a bucket already contained a value, the algorithm would have returned; therefore each active bucket has at most one stored value.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Python's \u003ccode>//\u003c/code> is floor division, which gives consistent contiguous buckets for negative values as well as positive ones. The buckets are not based on truncation towards zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Values [1, 5, 3], index limit 2, value limit 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>bucket width = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 1 into bucket 0; insert 5 into bucket 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window holds both values\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Value 3 is in bucket 1, already occupied by 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>|3 - 5| = 2; return \u003cmark>true\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If two integers are within \u003ccode>value_diff\u003c/code>, their bucket numbers differ by at most one: two buckets separated by an entire bucket have a minimum gap of \u003ccode>value_diff + 2\u003c/code>. Checking the same bucket and its two neighbours therefore considers every possible qualifying value. The maintained window ensures every considered index is close enough, so the returned match is valid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When no bucket matches, inserting the current value and evicting the oldest value preserves the invariant for the next index. By induction, no qualifying pair is missed. These bounds concern integer values; for real-valued inputs, bucket endpoints and precision require a different treatment.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When \u003ccode>value_diff\u003c/code> is zero, the bucket width is one, so two equal integers share a bucket and distinct integers do not. For a negative example with width three, \u003ccode>-1 // 3\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>0 // 3\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>0\u003c/code>; those adjacent buckets are still checked against each other. Their actual difference is one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def contains_nearby_almost_duplicate(nums, index_diff, value_diff):\n    if index_diff &lt; 0 or value_diff &lt; 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"distance limits must be non-negative\")\n    if index_diff == 0:\n        return False\n    width = value_diff + 1\n    buckets = {}\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        bucket = value // width\n        if bucket in buckets:\n            return True\n        if bucket - 1 in buckets and value - buckets[bucket - 1] &lt;= value_diff:\n            return True\n        if bucket + 1 in buckets and buckets[bucket + 1] - value &lt;= value_diff:\n            return True\n        buckets[bucket] = value\n        if i &gt;= index_diff:\n            del buckets[nums[i - index_diff] // width]\n    return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each value makes a constant number of expected O(1) dictionary operations, so expected time is O(n). The active window stores at most \u003ccode>min(n, index_diff)\u003c/code> values, giving O(min(n, index_diff)) auxiliary space. The expected bound relies on ordinary hash-table behaviour; it is not a worst-case guarantee against adversarial hashing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Width is \u003ccode>value_diff + 1\u003c/code>, including when the permitted value difference is zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use floor division for negative integers; truncation towards zero gives inconsistent bucket ranges.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Evict by index, not by comparing values, and check before inserting the current index.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not evict before checking the value exactly \u003ccode>index_diff\u003c/code> positions back; that pair is allowed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use an ordered multiset and check the smallest active value at least \u003ccode>x - value_diff\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the matching pair of indices by storing an index with each bucket value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count qualifying pairs instead of merely detecting one; a single value per bucket is no longer sufficient.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
